// Class 12 Physics Unit I: Electrostatics (JKBOSE / NCERT Official Syllabus - 09 Marks)
// Comprehensive Deep Reference Book Standard (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora) with 5-Layer Topic Breakdown

export const c12Phy1HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary & Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Terms for JKBOSE / NCERT Unit I: Electrostatics (09 Marks)</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>1. Electric Charge (q):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Fundamental scalar property of subatomic particles that causes electrostatic attraction or repulsion [Coulomb C, Dimensions: [A T]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>2. Coulomb's Law:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Fundamental inverse-square law of electrostatic force F = [1/(4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub> / r<sup>2</sup>) acting along the line of centers.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>3. Electric Field (<b>E</b>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Force experienced per unit positive infinitesimal test charge: <b>E</b> = <b>F</b> / q<sub>0</sub> [N C<sup>-1</sup> or V m<sup>-1</sup>, Dimensions: [M L T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>4. Electric Dipole Moment (<b>p</b>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Vector <b>p</b> = q (2<b>a</b>) directed from -q to +q [Coulomb-meter C m, Dimensions: [L T A]]. Torque: <b>&tau;</b> = <b>p</b> &times; <b>E</b>.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>5. Electric Flux (&Phi;<sub>E</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Total field lines passing normally through a surface: &Phi;<sub>E</sub> = &int;&int; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> [N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-1</sup> or V m, Dimensions: [M L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>6. Gauss's Law:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Total outward flux through any closed 3D Gaussian surface equals &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">1. Electric Charges, Conductors, Insulators &amp; Quantization of Charge</h2>\n  \n  <p>Electrostatics is the fundamental branch of electromagnetism that investigates the behavior, interactions, forces, fields, and potentials associated with stationary electric charges at rest. In classical physics, matter is composed of atoms containing positively charged protons (+e) within a dense nucleus and negatively charged electrons (-e) bound in surrounding orbitals. When the exact balance between protons and electrons is disrupted, macroscopic physical bodies acquire a net electric charge.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Electric Charge (q)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Electric charge is an intrinsic scalar physical property of elementary subatomic particles of matter that causes them to experience an attractive or repulsive electromagnetic force when placed in electromagnetic fields. SI Unit: Coulomb (C). Dimensional Formula: [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>1</sup> A<sup>1</sup>] = [A T].</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Fundamental Classification of Materials</h3>\n  <p>Materials in nature are broadly categorized based on the mobility of their electric charge carriers:</p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>Conductors:</b> Substances that contain abundant mobile free valence electrons (approximately 10<sup>28</sup> to 10<sup>29</sup> free electrons per m<sup>3</sup>) that can drift easily through the metal crystal lattice under the influence of an electric field (e.g. Copper, Silver, Aluminium, Human Body, Earth). When charge is transferred to a conductor, it instantly spreads across its entire outer surface.</li>\n    <li><b>Insulators (Dielectrics):</b> Substances in which all electrons are tightly bound to parent atomic nuclei by strong valence forces, leaving virtually zero free electrons for conduction (e.g. Glass, Mica, Quartz, Ebonite, Dry Wood, Plastics). When charge is given to an insulator, it remains strictly localized at the point of application.</li>\n    <li><b>Semiconductors:</b> Materials whose electrical conductivity lies intermediate between conductors and insulators (e.g. Silicon, Germanium).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Physical Methods of Charging</h3>\n  <p>A body can be charged electrically through three distinct physical mechanisms:</p>\n  <ol style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>Charging by Friction:</b> When two suitable dielectric materials are rubbed together, frictional thermal energy overcomes the work function of electrons in one body, transferring electrons to the other. The material with lower work function loses electrons and becomes positively charged (e.g., Glass rod rubbed with silk &rarr; Glass becomes positive, silk becomes negative).</li>\n    <li><b>Charging by Conduction:</b> When an uncharged conducting body is brought into direct physical contact with a charged conductor, mobile electrons flow between them until they reach electrostatic equilibrium. Both bodies acquire like charges.</li>\n    <li><b>Charging by Electrostatic Induction:</b> The process of charging a neutral conductor without any physical contact with a charged body. When a positively charged rod is brought near an insulated neutral sphere, free electrons are attracted toward the near face (leaving bound negative charge) while the far face is left with excess positive charge. When the far face is connected to Earth (earthing), electrons from Earth neutralize the positive charges. Removing the rod results in a uniformly distributed negative charge across the conductor.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(iii) Principle of Quantization of Charge</h3>\n  <p>Experimental studies conducted by Robert Millikan in his famous Oil Drop Experiment established that electric charge is not continuous, but exists in discrete packets or quanta.</p>\n  \n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Quantization of Charge</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">The Principle of Quantization of Charge states that the total electric charge (q) possessed by any physical body is always an integral multiple of the basic elementary quantum of charge carried by a single electron or proton (e = 1.602 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C).</div>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>q = &plusmn; n e &nbsp;|&nbsp; n &isin; {1, 2, 3, ...} &nbsp;|&nbsp; e = 1.602176 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C</b></div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 193, 7, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FFC107; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FFD54F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM FOCUS & COMMON TRAPS: Why is Quantization Ignored at Macroscopic Scale?</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">At the macroscopic level (e.g. microcoulombs or coulombs), the magnitude of charge involved is immense. For example, 1 &mu;C contains n = q/e = 10<sup>-6</sup> / (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>) &approx; 6.25 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> electrons. At such enormous numbers, the addition or subtraction of a few million electrons is undetectable, making charge appear perfectly continuous (like fluid flow), and quantization is practically ignored in everyday macroscopic circuits.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 229, 255, 0.05); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 229, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📝 SOLVED BOARD EXAMPLE 1:</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: 500; font-size: 15.5px; margin-bottom: 10px;\">A polythene piece rubbed with wool is found to have a negative charge of 3.2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> C. (a) Estimate the number of electrons transferred from wool to polythene. (b) Is there a transfer of mass from wool to polythene?</div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3px solid #00E5FF; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00E5FF;\">Step-by-Step Solution:</b><br><b>(a) Number of Electrons:</b><br>Using quantization formula q = n e:<br>n = q / e = (3.2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> C) / (1.602 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C) = <b>2.0 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> electrons</b>.<br><br><b>(b) Mass Transfer:</b><br>Yes, since electrons have mass (m<sub>e</sub> = 9.11 &times; 10<sup>-31</sup> kg), transfer of electrons transfers mass:<br>&Delta;m = n &times; m<sub>e</sub> = (2.0 &times; 10<sup>12</sup>) &times; (9.11 &times; 10<sup>-31</sup> kg) = <b>1.82 &times; 10<sup>-18</sup> kg</b>.<br>This mass increase is extremely tiny and practically undetectable, but theoretically real.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">2. Law of Conservation of Charge</h2>\n\n  <p>The Law of Conservation of Charge is one of the most fundamental universal conservation laws in modern physics, holding strictly across all microscopic, quantum, macroscopic, and astrophysical scales without exception.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Law of Conservation of Charge</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">The Law of Conservation of Charge states that the total algebraic sum of positive and negative electric charges in an electrically isolated physical system remains strictly constant over time, regardless of any physical, chemical, or high-energy nuclear processes occurring within the system.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>Charge can neither be created nor destroyed in isolation; it can only be transferred from one body to another or created in equal and opposite pairs. Key universal demonstrations include:</p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>Pair Production:</b> A high-energy gamma-ray photon (&gamma;) interacting with a heavy nucleus converts into an electron (e<sup>-</sup>) and a positron (e<sup>+</sup>):<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\">&gamma; (q = 0) &rarr; e<sup>-</sup> (-e) + e<sup>+</sup> (+e) &nbsp; [Net Initial = 0, Net Final = 0]</span></li>\n    <li><b>Electron-Positron Annihilation:</b> An electron and positron collide and annihilate each other, converting into two pure gamma photons:<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\">e<sup>-</sup> (-e) + e<sup>+</sup> (+e) &rarr; 2 &gamma; (q = 0) &nbsp; [Net Initial = 0, Net Final = 0]</span></li>\n    <li><b>Radioactive Alpha Decay:</b> Decay of Uranium-238 nucleus into Thorium-234 and an alpha particle:<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><sup>238</sup><sub>92</sub>U (+92e) &rarr; <sup>234</sup><sub>90</sub>Th (+90e) + <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He (+2e) &nbsp; [Initial = +92e, Final = +92e]</span></li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">3. Coulomb's Law, Superposition Principle &amp; Continuous Charge Distribution</h2>\n\n  <p>In 1785, French physicist Charles-Augustin de Coulomb quantified the electrostatic force between two stationary electric charges using a sensitive torsion balance.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Coulomb's Law</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Coulomb's Law states that the electrostatic force of attraction or repulsion between two stationary point charges is directly proportional to the scalar product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance separating them, acting along the straight line joining the centers of the two charges.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>Scalar Form: F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span>\n  </span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub>|</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span>\n  </span> = 8.98755 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-2</sup> &approx; 9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-2</sup></b></div>\n\n  <p>Here <b>&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = 8.8541878 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> C<sup>2</sup> N<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-2</sup> (or F m<sup>-1</sup>)</b> is the <i>absolute electrical permittivity of free space</i>. Its dimensional formula is <b>[M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-3</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>]</b>.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Vector Form of Coulomb's Law & Newton's Third Law\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Let position vectors of two point charges q<sub>1</sub> and q<sub>2</sub> with respect to origin O be <b>r</b><sub>1</sub> and <b>r</b><sub>2</sub>.<br>\n  Position vector of q<sub>2</sub> with respect to q<sub>1</sub> is <b>r</b><sub>21</sub> = <b>r</b><sub>2</sub> - <b>r</b><sub>1</sub>, with unit vector <b>r̂</b><sub>21</sub> = <b>r</b><sub>21</sub> / |<b>r</b><sub>21</sub>|.<br>\n  Position vector of q<sub>1</sub> with respect to q<sub>2</sub> is <b>r</b><sub>12</sub> = <b>r</b><sub>1</sub> - <b>r</b><sub>2</sub> = -<b>r</b><sub>21</sub>, with unit vector <b>r̂</b><sub>12</sub> = -<b>r̂</b><sub>21</sub>.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Force Exerted on q<sub>2</sub> by q<sub>1</sub>:</b><br>\n  <b>F</b><sub>21</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub>) / |<b>r</b><sub>21</sub>|<sup>2</sup> ] <b>r̂</b><sub>21</sub><br><br>\n  <b>3. Force Exerted on q<sub>1</sub> by q<sub>2</sub>:</b><br>\n  <b>F</b><sub>12</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub>) / |<b>r</b><sub>12</sub>|<sup>2</sup> ] <b>r̂</b><sub>12</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub>) / |<b>r</b><sub>21</sub>|<sup>2</sup> ] (-<b>r̂</b><sub>21</sub>) = -<b>F</b><sub>21</sub><br><br>\n  <b>Conclusion:</b> <b>F</b><sub>12</sub> = -<b>F</b><sub>21</sub>.<br>\n  This proves that electrostatic forces between two charges are equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, and collinear, obeying <b>Newton's Third Law of Motion</b>.\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Dielectric Constant / Relative Permittivity (K or &epsilon;<sub>r</sub>)</h3>\n  <p>When the two charges are placed in a material medium of absolute permittivity &epsilon; instead of vacuum, the force decreases due to polarization of the surrounding molecules:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>F<sub>med</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; &epsilon;</span>\n  </span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F<sub>vac</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">K</span>\n  </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; K = &epsilon;<sub>r</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F<sub>vac</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F<sub>med</sub></span>\n  </span></b></div>\n  <p>Dielectric constant K is a dimensionless quantity. For vacuum K = 1, for dry air K &approx; 1.00054, for pure water K = 80.4, and for perfect conductors/metals <b>K = &infin;</b>.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Principle of Superposition &amp; Continuous Charge Distributions</h3>\n  <p>When multiple point charges interact, the net electrostatic force acting on any given test charge q<sub>0</sub> is the vector sum of individual forces exerted on it by all other charges independently:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>F<sub>net</sub> = F<sub>1</sub> + F<sub>2</sub> + F<sub>3</sub> + ... + F<sub>N</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sub>0</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span>\n  </span> &Sigma;<sub>i=1</sub><sup>N</sup> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sub>i</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>i0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> r̂<sub>i0</sub></b></div>\n\n  <p>For macroscopic objects where charges are continuously distributed:</p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>Linear Charge Density (&lambda;):</b> Charge per unit length along a thin wire: &lambda; = dq / dl [C m<sup>-1</sup>]. Force: <b>F</b> = [q<sub>0</sub> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] &int; [&lambda; dl / r<sup>2</sup>] <b>r̂</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>Surface Charge Density (&sigma;):</b> Charge per unit surface area: &sigma; = dq / dA [C m<sup>-2</sup>]. Force: <b>F</b> = [q<sub>0</sub> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] &int;&int; [&sigma; dA / r<sup>2</sup>] <b>r̂</b>.</li>\n    <li><b>Volume Charge Density (&rho;):</b> Charge per unit 3D volume: &rho; = dq / dV [C m<sup>-3</sup>]. Force: <b>F</b> = [q<sub>0</sub> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] &int;&int;&int; [&rho; dV / r<sup>2</sup>] <b>r̂</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 16px; padding: 20px; margin: 28px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 16px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 1. COULOMB'S LAW & SUPERPOSITION PRINCIPLE\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 920 520\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qRedGlow1\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qCyanGlow1\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#38BDF8\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qGoldGlow1\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FDE047\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#EAB308\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"mRed1\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mCyan1\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mGreen1\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mYellow1\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#FFD700\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. COULOMB'S LAW (VECTOR FORM)</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"400\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94A3B8\"/>\n    <text x=\"45\" y=\"425\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">O (Origin)</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"140\" r=\"22\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow1)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">q1</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"270\" r=\"22\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow1)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"340\" y=\"276\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">q2</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"65\" y1=\"400\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"158\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mYellow1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"90\" y=\"270\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">r1</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"65\" y1=\"400\" x2=\"322\" y2=\"282\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mYellow1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"365\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">r2</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"170\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"322\" y2=\"256\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">r21 = r2 - r1</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"170\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">F12 = -F21</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"415\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"365\" y=\"355\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">F21</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 1.1: Coulomb's Law in Vector Form</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"460\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"495\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(470, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">SUPERPOSITION PRINCIPLE</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"250\" r=\"22\" fill=\"url(#qGoldGlow1)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"180\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#0B0F19\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">+q0</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"90\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">q1</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"380\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"65\" y=\"385\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">q2</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"380\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"340\" y=\"385\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">q3</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"350\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"160\" y=\"335\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">F1</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">F2</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">F3</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"270\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"315\" y=\"125\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">F_net = Sum Fi</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 1.2: Superposition Principle</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Fig 1.1: Vector form of Coulomb's Law (F<sub>12</sub> = -F<sub>21</sub>) & Fig 1.2: Superposition Principle showing vector addition of forces on charge q<sub>0</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">4. Electric Field, Field Lines &amp; Dipole Fields (Axial &amp; Equatorial)</h2>\n\n  <p>The concept of an Electric Field was introduced by Michael Faraday to explain action-at-a-distance. A source charge Q modifies the surrounding space by establishing an electrostatic field <b>E</b>. When a test charge q<sub>0</sub> enters this region, it experiences an electrostatic force <b>F</b> = q<sub>0</sub> <b>E</b>.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Electric Field Intensity (<b>E</b>)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Electric Field Intensity at any point in space is defined as the electrostatic force experienced per unit infinitesimal positive test charge placed at that point: <b>E</b> = lim<sub>q<sub>0</sub> &rarr; 0</sub> (<b>F</b> / q<sub>0</sub>). SI Unit: N C<sup>-1</sup> or V m<sup>-1</sup>. Dimensional Formula: [M L T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>].</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>For an isolated source charge Q in vacuum at distance r:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>E = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span>\n  </span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Q</span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> r̂ &nbsp; (Radially outwards if Q &gt; 0, radially inwards if Q &lt; 0)</b></div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Properties of Electric Field Lines</h3>\n  <ol style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>Origin and Termination:</b> Field lines originate continuously from positive charges and terminate perpendicularly on negative charges (or extend to infinity).</li>\n    <li><b>Direction of Field:</b> The tangent drawn to an electric field line at any point indicates the direction of resultant electric field vector <b>E</b> at that point.</li>\n    <li><b>Non-Intersection Rule:</b> Two electric field lines can NEVER intersect each other. If they did, two tangents could be drawn at the intersection point, implying two distinct directions of electric field at a single point, which is physically impossible.</li>\n    <li><b>No Closed Loops:</b> Electric field lines never form closed loops. This fundamental property reflects the conservative nature of the electrostatic field (work done along closed loop &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = 0).</li>\n    <li><b>Lateral Repulsion & Longitudinal Contraction:</b> Field lines tend to contract longitudinally (explaining attraction between unlike charges) and exert lateral pressure on each other (explaining repulsion between like charges).</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 16px; padding: 20px; margin: 28px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 16px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 2. ELECTRIC FIELD PATTERNS\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 920 520\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qRedGlow2\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qCyanGlow2\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#38BDF8\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"mRed2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" fill=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mCyan2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" fill=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mGold2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" fill=\"#FFD700\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"890\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"445\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. ELECTRIC FIELD PATTERNS &amp; FIELD LINES</text>\n\n    <g transform=\"translate(15, 55)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"200\" height=\"410\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.06)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"100\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">Isolated +q</text>\n      <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"200\" r=\"22\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow2)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"100\" y=\"207\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\">+</text>\n\n      <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"178\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"315\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"78\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"122\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n\n      <line x1=\"84\" y1=\"184\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"116\" y1=\"216\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"116\" y1=\"184\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"84\" y1=\"216\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed2)\"/>\n\n      <text x=\"100\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Radially Outward</text>\n    </g>\n\n    <g transform=\"translate(230, 55)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"200\" height=\"410\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.06)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"100\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">Isolated -q</text>\n      <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"200\" r=\"22\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow2)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"100\" y=\"207\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"24\" font-weight=\"bold\">-</text>\n\n      <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"315\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"15\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"78\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"122\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n\n      <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"84\" y2=\"184\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"170\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"116\" y2=\"216\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"170\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"116\" y2=\"184\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n      <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"84\" y2=\"216\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan2)\"/>\n\n      <text x=\"100\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Radially Inward</text>\n    </g>\n\n    <g transform=\"translate(445, 55)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"210\" height=\"410\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.06)\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"105\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">Electric Dipole</text>\n      <circle cx=\"55\" cy=\"200\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow2)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"55\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">+</text>\n\n      <circle cx=\"155\" cy=\"200\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow2)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"155\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\">-</text>\n\n      <line x1=\"73\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"137\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mGold2)\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 55 182 Q 105 125 155 182\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 55 218 Q 105 275 155 218\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 55 182 Q 105 75 155 182\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 55 218 Q 105 325 155 218\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n\n      <text x=\"105\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Curved + to -</text>\n    </g>\n\n    <g transform=\"translate(670, 55)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"205\" height=\"410\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.06)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"102\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">Like Charges</text>\n      <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"200\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow2)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"50\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">+</text>\n\n      <circle cx=\"155\" cy=\"200\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow2)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <text x=\"155\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">+</text>\n\n      <path d=\"M 50 182 Q 80 140 85 85\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 50 218 Q 80 260 85 315\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 155 182 Q 125 140 120 85\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 155 218 Q 125 260 120 315\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n\n      <circle cx=\"102\" cy=\"200\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n      <text x=\"102\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">N</text>\n      <text x=\"102\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Neutral Point (E = 0)</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Field line geometry for Isolated +q (radially outward), Isolated -q (radially inward), Electric Dipole (+ to -), and Like Charges (+ and +, displaying central Neutral Point N).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Electric Dipole &amp; Dipole Moment</h3>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Electric Dipole & Dipole Moment (<b>p</b>)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">An Electric Dipole is a pair of equal and opposite point charges (+q and -q) separated by a small distance 2a. The Electric Dipole Moment vector <b>p</b> is defined as the product of charge magnitude q and separation vector 2<b>a</b>: <b>p</b> = q (2<b>a</b>). Its SI unit is Coulomb-meter (C m) and it points from negative charge (-q) to positive charge (+q).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Electric Field on Axial Line (End-on Position)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider an electric dipole consisting of charges -q at point A and +q at point B separated by distance 2a. Center of dipole is O.<br>\n  Let P be an axial point on the axis line at distance r from center O.<br>\n  Distance of P from +q is BP = (r - a).<br>\n  Distance of P from -q is AP = (r + a).<br><br>\n  <b>2. Individual Electric Fields at P:</b><br>\n  - Field due to +q: E<sub>+</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ q / (r - a)<sup>2</sup> ] (directed along BP away from dipole).<br>\n  - Field due to -q: E<sub>-</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ q / (r + a)<sup>2</sup> ] (directed along PA towards dipole).<br><br>\n  <b>3. Net Axial Field:</b><br>\n  Since E<sub>+</sub> &gt; E<sub>-</sub>, resultant field points along direction of dipole moment <b>p</b>:<br>\n  E<sub>axial</sub> = E<sub>+</sub> - E<sub>-</sub> = [ q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 1 / (r - a)<sup>2</sup> - 1 / (r + a)<sup>2</sup> ]<br>\n  E<sub>axial</sub> = [ q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (r + a)<sup>2</sup> - (r - a)<sup>2</sup> ] / [ (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ]<br>\n  E<sub>axial</sub> = [ q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 4 a r ] / [ (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ] = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 2 (q &times; 2a) r ] / [ (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ]<br><br>\n  Substitute dipole moment p = q (2a):<br>\n  <b>E<sub>axial</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 2 p r / (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ]</b><br><br>\n  <b>4. Short Dipole Approximation (r &gt;&gt; a):</b><br>\n  Neglecting a<sup>2</sup> compared to r<sup>2</sup>:<br>\n  <b>E<sub>axial</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 2 p / r<sup>3</sup> ] &nbsp; (Strictly Parallel to <b>p</b>)</b>\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Electric Field on Equatorial Line (Broadside-on Position)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider dipole -q at A and +q at B separated by 2a with center O. Let Q be a point on the perpendicular bisector at distance r from O.<br>\n  Distance of Q from each charge: AQ = BQ = &radic;(r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>).<br><br>\n  <b>2. Individual Field Magnitudes:</b><br>\n  E<sub>+</sub> = E<sub>-</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ q / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>) ].<br><br>\n  <b>3. Resolution into Components:</b><br>\n  - Vertical components E<sub>+</sub> sin &theta; (upward) and E<sub>-</sub> sin &theta; (downward) are equal and opposite, hence cancel out completely.<br>\n  - Horizontal components E<sub>+</sub> cos &theta; and E<sub>-</sub> cos &theta; act in the SAME direction (opposite to <b>p</b>):<br>\n  E<sub>eq</sub> = E<sub>+</sub> cos &theta; + E<sub>-</sub> cos &theta; = 2 E<sub>+</sub> cos &theta;<br><br>\n  From right triangle &Delta;OBQ: cos &theta; = Base / Hypotenuse = a / &radic;(r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>).<br>\n  E<sub>eq</sub> = 2 [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ q / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>) ] [ a / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>1/2</sup> ]<br>\n  E<sub>eq</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (q &times; 2a) / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup> ] = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ p / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup> ]<br><br>\n  <b>4. Short Dipole Approximation (r &gt;&gt; a):</b><br>\n  <b>E<sub>eq</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ p / r<sup>3</sup> ] &nbsp; (Strictly Anti-parallel to <b>p</b>)</b><br><br>\n  <b>5. Comparison Ratio:</b><br>\n  <b>E<sub>axial</sub> / E<sub>eq</sub> = 2 : 1</b> (At identical distance r from short dipole).\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 16px; padding: 20px; margin: 28px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 16px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 3. ELECTRIC DIPOLE FIELDS (AXIAL & EQUATORIAL)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 920 520\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qRedGlow3\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qCyanGlow3\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#38BDF8\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"mRed3\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mCyan3\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mGreen3\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mGold3\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#FFD700\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. ELECTRIC DIPOLE (AXIAL POINT)</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"410\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#475569\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,5\"/>\n\n    <circle cx=\"85\" cy=\"230\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow3)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"85\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">-q</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"230\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow3)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"195\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">+q</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"230\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#FFD700\"/>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"275\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"275\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"280\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"195\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"280\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">2a</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"95\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#mGold3)\"/>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">p (vector)</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"330\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <text x=\"250\" y=\"355\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Distance r</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"230\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    <text x=\"360\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"360\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen3)\"/>\n    <text x=\"360\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">E_axial || p</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 3.1: Field at Axial Point (r &gt;&gt; a)</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"460\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"495\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(470, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">EQUATORIAL POINT RESOLUTION</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"360\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"360\" stroke=\"#475569\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"360\" r=\"16\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow3)\"/>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"365\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">-q</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"360\" r=\"16\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow3)\"/>\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"365\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">+q</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"95\" y1=\"330\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mGold3)\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">p</text>\n\n    <g transform=\"translate(180, 200)\">\n      <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"-80\" y2=\"-80\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan3)\"/>\n      <text x=\"-95\" y=\"-90\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">q cos &#952;</text>\n\n      <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"-80\" stroke=\"#38BDF8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan3)\"/>\n      <text x=\"50\" y=\"-90\" fill=\"#38BDF8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">E_equal</text>\n\n      <path d=\"M 0 -30 A 30 30 0 0 0 -22 -22\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n      <text x=\"-15\" y=\"-40\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">&#952;</text>\n\n      <path d=\"M 0 -30 A 30 30 0 0 1 22 -22\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n      <text x=\"15\" y=\"-40\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">&#952;</text>\n\n      <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"-120\" y2=\"0\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3.8\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed3)\"/>\n      <text x=\"-60\" y=\"25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">q cos &#952;</text>\n\n      <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n      <text x=\"15\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text>\n    </g>\n\n    <rect x=\"230\" y=\"380\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.12)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"320\" y=\"406\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">E_equatorial || -p</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 3.2: Field at Equatorial Point (r &gt;&gt; a)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Fig 3.1: Axial Field E<sub>axial</sub> (parallel to <b>p</b>) & Fig 3.2: Equatorial Field resolution showing cancellation of sin components and addition of cos components (anti-parallel to <b>p</b>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">5. Torque on a Dipole in Uniform Electric Field &amp; Electric Flux</h2>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Torque on Electric Dipole in Uniform Field\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider an electric dipole of moment <b>p</b> placed in a uniform electric field <b>E</b> at an angle &theta;.<br>\n  - Force on +q: <b>F</b><sub>+</sub> = +q <b>E</b> (along <b>E</b>).<br>\n  - Force on -q: <b>F</b><sub>-</sub> = -q <b>E</b> (opposite to <b>E</b>).<br>\n  Net translational force: <b>F</b><sub>net</sub> = +q<b>E</b> + (-q<b>E</b>) = 0.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Calculation of Couple (Torque):</b><br>\n  Since the two forces are equal, opposite, and act along different lines of action, they constitute a couple.<br>\n  Perpendicular lever arm distance between action lines = 2a sin &theta;.<br>\n  Torque magnitude &tau; = Force &times; Perpendicular distance = (q E) &times; (2a sin &theta;) = (q &times; 2a) E sin &theta;.<br>\n  <b>&tau; = p E sin &theta; &nbsp;|&nbsp; In Vector Form: <b>&tau;</b> = <b>p</b> &times; <b>E</b></b><br><br>\n  <b>3. Equilibrium Conditions:</b><br>\n  - <b>Stable Equilibrium (&theta; = 0&deg;):</b> Torque &tau; = 0, Potential Energy U = -pE (minimum). Dipole aligns parallel to <b>E</b>.<br>\n  - <b>Unstable Equilibrium (&theta; = 180&deg;):</b> Torque &tau; = 0, Potential Energy U = +pE (maximum). Dipole aligned anti-parallel to <b>E</b>.<br>\n  - <b>Maximum Torque (&theta; = 90&deg;):</b> &tau;<sub>max</sub> = p E.\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Electric Flux (&Phi;<sub>E</sub>)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Electric Flux through an elementary surface area dA in an electric field is the scalar dot product of electric field <b>E</b> and area vector d<b>A</b>: d&Phi;<sub>E</sub> = <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = E dA cos &theta;. Total flux through surface A is: &Phi;<sub>E</sub> = &int;&int; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b>. SI Unit: N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-1</sup> or Volt meter (V m).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 16px; padding: 20px; margin: 28px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 16px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 4. TORQUE ON DIPOLE & ELECTRIC FLUX\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 920 520\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qRedGlow4\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qCyanGlow4\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#38BDF8\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"mRed4\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mCyan4\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mGreen4\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. TORQUE ON A DIPOLE</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"310\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"310\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <text x=\"385\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\">E (Field)</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"320\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n\n    <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"320\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qCyanGlow4)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">-q</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"160\" r=\"18\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow4)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"280\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">+q</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 170 320 A 50 50 0 0 0 160 280\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"180\" y=\"305\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">&#952;</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"280\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"365\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen4)\"/>\n    <text x=\"330\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">+qE</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"320\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"320\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen4)\"/>\n    <text x=\"45\" y=\"345\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">-qE</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"280\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"320\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"320\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"320\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <text x=\"295\" y=\"245\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">2a sin &#952;</text>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">&#964; = p &times; E | &#964; = p E sin &#952;</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 4.1: Torque on a Dipole in Uniform Field</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"460\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"495\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(470, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">ELECTRIC FLUX (Phi_E)</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 50 160 Q 200 190 380 120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 50 210 Q 200 230 380 200\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 50 260 Q 200 260 380 270\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 50 310 Q 200 290 380 340\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" marker-end=\"url(#mCyan4)\"/>\n    <text x=\"390\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\">E (Lines)</text>\n\n    <polygon points=\"170,170 250,140 275,300 195,330\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.35)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n\n    <line x1=\"222\" y1=\"235\" x2=\"315\" y2=\"185\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen4)\"/>\n    <text x=\"325\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\">dA</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 265 210 A 25 25 0 0 0 275 228\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"285\" y=\"235\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">&#952;</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"40\" y=\"380\" width=\"355\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"412\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">Formula: &#934;_E = &#8747; E &middot; dA = &#8747; E dA cos &#952;</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 4.2: Electric Flux</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Fig 4.1: Torque couple acting on dipole (<b>&tau;</b> = <b>p</b> &times; <b>E</b>) & Fig 4.2: Electric Flux &Phi;<sub>E</sub> through 3D area patch dA.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">6. Gauss's Theorem &amp; Its 3 Core Applications</h2>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Gauss's Theorem</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Gauss's Theorem states that the total outward electric flux &Phi;<sub>E</sub> through any closed 3D Gaussian surface in free space is equal to 1/&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> times the net algebraic electric charge enclosed inside the surface: &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Application 1: Infinitely Long Straight Uniformly Charged Wire\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider an infinitely long thin wire carrying uniform linear charge density &lambda; = dq/dl.<br>\n  By cylindrical symmetry, electric field <b>E</b> is directed radially outwards.<br>\n  Construct a coaxial cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius r and length L.<br>\n  Enclosed charge: q<sub>enclosed</sub> = &lambda; L.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Flux through 3 Surface Segments:</b><br>\n  - Top Flat End (S<sub>1</sub>): <b>E</b> &perp; d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>1</sub> = &int; E dA cos 90&deg; = 0.<br>\n  - Bottom Flat End (S<sub>2</sub>): <b>E</b> &perp; d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>2</sub> = &int; E dA cos 90&deg; = 0.<br>\n  - Curved Cylindrical Surface (S<sub>3</sub>): <b>E</b> || d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>3</sub> = &int; E dA cos 0&deg; = E &int; dA = E (2&pi; r L).<br><br>\n  <b>3. Applying Gauss's Law:</b><br>\n  &Phi;<sub>total</sub> = 0 + 0 + E (2&pi; r L) = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><br>\n  E (2&pi; r L) = &lambda; L / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><br>\n  <b>E = \" + frac(\"&lambda;\", \"2&pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r\") + \" &nbsp; (E &prop; 1 / r)</b>\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Application 2: Uniformly Charged Infinite Plane Sheet\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider an infinite thin non-conducting plane sheet with uniform surface charge density &sigma; = dq/dA.<br>\n  Electric field lines emerge perpendicularly outwards from both sides.<br>\n  Construct a cylindrical pillbox Gaussian surface of cross-sectional area A piercing normally through the sheet.<br>\n  Enclosed charge: q<sub>enclosed</sub> = &sigma; A.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Flux Calculation:</b><br>\n  - Curved Surface: <b>E</b> || surface &rArr; &Phi;<sub>curved</sub> = 0.<br>\n  - Left Flat Cap: <b>E</b> || d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>left</sub> = E A.<br>\n  - Right Flat Cap: <b>E</b> || d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>right</sub> = E A.<br>\n  Total Flux &Phi;<sub>total</sub> = E A + E A = 2 E A.<br><br>\n  <b>3. Applying Gauss's Law:</b><br>\n  2 E A = &sigma; A / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <b>E = \" + frac(\"&sigma;\", \"2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>\") + \" &nbsp; (Uniform, completely independent of distance r)</b><br><br>\n  <b>Case of Two Oppositely Charged Sheets (+&sigma; and -&sigma;):</b><br>\n  - Between the sheets: E<sub>net</sub> = &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) + &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = <b>&sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></b>.<br>\n  - Outside the sheets: E<sub>net</sub> = &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) - &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = <b>0</b>.\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Application 3: Uniformly Charged Thin Spherical Shell (Radius R, Charge Q)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Case I: Outside the Shell (r &ge; R):</b><br>\n  Construct a concentric spherical Gaussian surface of radius r &ge; R.<br>\n  By spherical symmetry, <b>E</b> is uniform and radial: &Phi; = &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = E (4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>).<br>\n  Enclosed charge q<sub>enclosed</sub> = Q.<br>\n  By Gauss's Law: E (4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>) = Q / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <b>E<sub>out</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ Q / r<sup>2</sup> ]</b>.<br>\n  (For points outside, the entire charge behaves as if concentrated at the center).<br><br>\n  <b>2. Case II: At the Surface (r = R):</b><br>\n  <b>E<sub>surface</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ Q / R<sup>2</sup> ] = &sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></b>.<br><br>\n  <b>3. Case III: Inside the Shell (r &lt; R):</b><br>\n  Construct a concentric spherical Gaussian surface of radius r &lt; R.<br>\n  Since all charges reside exclusively on the outer surface of the shell, enclosed charge is strictly zero (q<sub>enclosed</sub> = 0).<br>\n  By Gauss's Law: E (4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>) = 0 / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <b>E<sub>in</sub> = 0</b>.<br>\n  This fundamental result is the basis of <b>Electrostatic Shielding</b>.\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 16px; padding: 20px; margin: 28px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 16px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 5. GAUSS'S THEOREM APPLICATIONS\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 920 520\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qRedGlow5\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"mRed5\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mCyan5\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"mGreen5\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">5. GAUSS'S THEOREM (LONG WIRE)</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"395\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"6\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"210\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"210\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"210\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"320\" cy=\"210\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\"/>\n    <text x=\"390\" y=\"235\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">+&#955;</text>\n\n    <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"210\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"320\" cy=\"210\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"300\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"300\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"295\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"305\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"295\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"305\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"210\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Length L</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"185\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">r</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"210\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#mGreen5)\"/>\n    <text x=\"210\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">E || dA</text>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">E || dA only on curved surface</text>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"410\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\">E = &#955; / (2&#960;&#949;0 r)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 5.1: Long Wire (&#955;)</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"460\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"495\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(470, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">SPHERICAL SHELL (r &gt; R &amp; r &lt; R)</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"205\" r=\"95\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"205\" r=\"85\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.06)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">+++++ Charged Shell (Q) +++++</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"217\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"185\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">R</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"205\" r=\"50\" fill=\"rgba(118,255,3,0.08)\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\"/>\n    <line x1=\"217\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#76FF03\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"190\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">r</text>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">E_in = 0</text>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">(q_enclosed = 0)</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"205\" r=\"130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,5\"/>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">E_out = [ 1 / (4&#960;&#949;0) ] (Q / r&#178;)</text>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"410\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">E_in = 0 (Inside Shell)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 5.2: Spherical Shell (r &gt; R and r &lt; R)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Fig 5.1: Infinitely Long Wire E = &lambda; / (2&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r) & Fig 5.2: Spherical Shell showing E<sub>out</sub> = Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>2</sup>) and E<sub>in</sub> = 0 inside the cavity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">7. Electric Potential &amp; Potential Difference</h2>\n\n  <p>Just as a mass in a gravitational field possesses gravitational potential energy due to conservative gravitational forces, stationary charges in an electrostatic field possess electrostatic potential energy. Electric potential is the scalar energy counterpart to the vector electric field <b>E</b>.</p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Electric Potential (V)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Electric Potential V at a point in an electrostatic field is defined as the work done by an external agent per unit positive infinitesimal test charge in slowly bringing it from infinity to that point against electrostatic forces without acceleration: V = W<sub>&infin;&rarr;P</sub> / q<sub>0</sub>. SI Unit: Volt (V = J C<sup>-1</sup>). Dimensional Formula: [M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>].</div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Electric Potential Difference (&Delta;V)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Electric Potential Difference between two points A and B is defined as the work done per unit positive test charge in carrying it from point A to point B: V<sub>B</sub> - V<sub>A</sub> = W<sub>A&rarr;B</sub> / q<sub>0</sub> = -&int;<sub>A</sub><sup>B</sup> <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Relation between Electric Field E and Potential Gradient dV/dr\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider two closely situated equipotential surfaces A and B separated by small perpendicular distance dr, having potentials V and (V - dV).<br><br>\n  <b>2. Work Done:</b> Small work done dW in moving unit positive test charge (+1 C) from surface B to A against electric field <b>E</b> is:<br>\n  dW = <b>F</b> &sdot; d<b>r</b> = (q<sub>0</sub> E) dr cos 180&deg; = -(1) E dr = -E dr.<br><br>\n  <b>3. Equating with Potential Difference:</b><br>\n  By definition: dW = V<sub>A</sub> - V<sub>B</sub> = V - (V - dV) = dV.<br>\n  Therefore: -E dr = dV &rArr; <b>E = - (dV / dr)</b>.<br><br>\n  <b>Physical Interpretation of Negative Sign:</b><br>\n  1. The electric field vector <b>E</b> always points in the direction of steepest rate of decrease in electrostatic potential.<br>\n  2. The magnitude of electric field equals the spatial rate of change of potential (potential gradient).\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">8. Potential due to a Point Charge, Dipole &amp; System of Charges</h2>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Electric Potential due to an Isolated Point Charge Q\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider an isolated positive point charge +Q at origin O in vacuum. We calculate potential V at point P at distance r from O.<br>\n  Let a unit test charge (+1 C) be at intermediate point A at distance x from O.<br>\n  Electrostatic repulsive force on +1 C is: F = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ Q / x<sup>2</sup> ] (directed along OA away from O).<br><br>\n  <b>2. Small Work Done:</b> Work dW in moving test charge through displacement dx towards O against force F is:<br>\n  dW = <b>F</b> &sdot; d<b>x</b> = F dx cos 180&deg; = -F dx = - [ Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> x<sup>2</sup>) ] dx.<br><br>\n  <b>3. Total Work Done from &infin; to r:</b><br>\n  V = W = &int;<sub>&infin;</sub><sup>r</sup> - [ Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> x<sup>2</sup>) ] dx = - [ Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ -1/x ]<sub>&infin;</sub><sup>r</sup> = [ Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 1/r - 1/&infin; ]<br><br>\n  <b>V = \" + frac(\"1\", \"4&pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>\") + \" \" + frac(\"Q\", \"r\") + \" &nbsp; (V &prop; 1 / r)</b>\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Electric Potential due to an Electric Dipole at General Point (r, &theta;)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider dipole charges -q at A and +q at B separated by 2a with center O. Let P be a point with polar coordinates (r, &theta;), where r &gt;&gt; a.<br>\n  Distances of P from +q and -q are r<sub>1</sub> &approx; r - a cos &theta; and r<sub>2</sub> &approx; r + a cos &theta;.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Algebraic Potential Sum:</b><br>\n  V = V<sub>+</sub> + V<sub>-</sub> = [ q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 1 / r<sub>1</sub> - 1 / r<sub>2</sub> ] = [ q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ 1 / (r - a cos &theta;) - 1 / (r + a cos &theta;) ]<br>\n  V = [ q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (2 a cos &theta;) / (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;) ]<br><br>\n  Substitute dipole moment p = q (2a) and neglect a<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta; for r &gt;&gt; a:<br>\n  <b>V = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (p cos &theta;) / r<sup>2</sup> ]</b><br><br>\n  <b>3. Special Cases:</b><br>\n  - <b>Axial Line (&theta; = 0&deg;):</b> cos 0&deg; = 1 &rArr; <b>V<sub>axial</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ p / r<sup>2</sup> ]</b>.<br>\n  - <b>Equatorial Line (&theta; = 90&deg;):</b> cos 90&deg; = 0 &rArr; <b>V<sub>eq</sub> = 0</b>. (Zero potential at all equatorial points).\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 9 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">9. Equipotential Surfaces &amp; Electrostatic Potential Energy</h2>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Equipotential Surface</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">An Equipotential Surface is any continuous locus of spatial points where the electrostatic potential has the exact same uniform constant value throughout (V = constant).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Fundamental Properties of Equipotential Surfaces</h3>\n  <ol style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>Zero Work Done:</b> The work done in moving any test charge q<sub>0</sub> between any two points A and B on an equipotential surface is strictly zero: W = q<sub>0</sub> (V<sub>B</sub> - V<sub>A</sub>) = q<sub>0</sub> (0) = 0.</li>\n    <li><b>Normal Electric Field Lines:</b> Since dW = <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = E dl cos &theta; = 0, cos &theta; = 0 &rArr; &theta; = 90&deg;. Electric field lines are ALWAYS strictly perpendicular to equipotential surfaces at every point.</li>\n    <li><b>Non-Intersection Principle:</b> Two equipotential surfaces can NEVER intersect. If they did, two different potential values would exist at the line of intersection, which is physically impossible.</li>\n    <li><b>Surface Spacing &amp; Field Strength:</b> From E = -dV/dr &rArr; dr = -dV/E. For a constant potential step dV, the spacing dr between adjacent equipotentials is inversely proportional to electric field strength (surfaces are crowded in strong field regions).</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Electrostatic Potential Energy of System of Charges\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. System of Two Point Charges:</b><br>\n  - Work in bringing q<sub>1</sub> to <b>r</b><sub>1</sub> in empty space: W<sub>1</sub> = 0.<br>\n  - Potential at <b>r</b><sub>2</sub> due to q<sub>1</sub> is V<sub>1</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ q<sub>1</sub> / r<sub>12</sub> ].<br>\n  - Work in bringing q<sub>2</sub>: W<sub>2</sub> = q<sub>2</sub> V<sub>1</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub>) / r<sub>12</sub> ].<br>\n  <b>U = W<sub>1</sub> + W<sub>2</sub> = [ 1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) ] [ (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub>) / r<sub>12</sub> ]</b><br><br>\n  <b>2. Electric Dipole in External Field E:</b><br>\n  Work done in rotating dipole from &theta;<sub>0</sub> = 90&deg; (reference zero energy) to &theta;:<br>\n  U = &int;<sub>90&deg;</sub><sup>&theta;</sup> &tau; d&theta; = &int;<sub>90&deg;</sub><sup>&theta;</sup> p E sin &theta; d&theta; = p E [-cos &theta;]<sub>90&deg;</sub><sup>&theta;</sup> = -p E cos &theta;.<br>\n  <b>U = - p E cos &theta; = - <b>p</b> &sdot; <b>E</b></b>\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 16px; padding: 20px; margin: 28px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 16px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 6. EQUIPOTENTIAL SURFACES & POTENTIAL\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 920 520\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qRedGlow6\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">6. EQUIPOTENTIAL SURFACES (POINT +q)</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"230\" r=\"140\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.03)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,5\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">V3 = 10 V</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"230\" r=\"95\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.06)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,5\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">V2 = 20 V</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"230\" r=\"50\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.1)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,5\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">V1 = 40 V</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"217\" cy=\"230\" r=\"22\" fill=\"url(#qRedGlow6)\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">+q</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"217\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"217\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"217\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"217\" y2=\"390\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"192\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"242\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"415\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">E is always Normal to Equipotential Surface</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 6.1: Concentric Equipotential Spheres</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"460\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"495\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(470, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">EQUIPOTENTIALS IN UNIFORM FIELD</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"300\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"300\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#mRed1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"385\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\">E (Field)</text>\n\n    <polygon points=\"100,80 140,60 140,360 100,380\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.15)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">V1 = 60V</text>\n\n    <polygon points=\"200,80 240,60 240,360 200,380\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.15)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">V2 = 40V</text>\n\n    <polygon points=\"300,80 340,60 340,360 300,380\" fill=\"rgba(0,229,255,0.15)\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"320\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">V3 = 20V</text>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"435\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">E = -dV / dr (Potential Decreases along E)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 6.2: Equipotential Planes in Uniform Field</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Fig 6.1: Concentric spherical equipotentials for isolated charge +q & Fig 6.2: Equidistant parallel equipotential planes in a uniform field (E = -dV/dr).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 10 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">10. Conductors, Insulators, Free &amp; Bound Charges</h2>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Free Charges vs Bound Charges Inside Conductors</h3>\n  <p>In metallic conductors (e.g. Copper, Silver), valence electrons easily detach from parent atoms, creating a sea of <b>free charges</b> (free electrons) that drift freely under electric forces. The remaining heavy positive atomic nuclei and core electrons remain rigidly locked in the crystal lattice, forming <b>bound charges</b> that cannot move.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) 6 Electrostatic Properties of Conductors</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>1. Electric Field Inside Conductor is Zero (E<sub>in</sub> = 0):</b> When placed in external field <b>E</b><sub>0</sub>, free electrons drift to the surface, establishing an induced internal field <b>E</b><sub>ind</sub> that exactly cancels <b>E</b><sub>0</sub> until <b>E</b><sub>net</sub> = 0.</li>\n    <li><b>2. Electric Field at Surface is Strictly Normal (E = &sigma;/&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>):</b> If a tangential component existed, surface electrons would experience force and flow, violating electrostatic equilibrium.</li>\n    <li><b>3. Static Charge Resides Entirely on Outer Surface:</b> By Gauss's Law, enclosing any interior volume yields &Phi; = &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = 0 &rArr; q<sub>enclosed</sub> = 0.</li>\n    <li><b>4. Potential is Constant Throughout Volume:</b> Since E = -dV/dr = 0 everywhere inside, V is constant and equal to surface potential.</li>\n    <li><b>5. Electrostatic Shielding:</b> The electric field inside any hollow metallic cavity is strictly zero (E = 0), regardless of external charge or field. Delicate electronic instruments and coaxial cables use this principle to shield against external noise.</li>\n    <li><b>6. Action of Points (Corona Discharge):</b> At sharp edges and pointed tips, radius of curvature r is very small, resulting in huge surface charge density (&sigma; &prop; 1/r) and extremely strong localized electric fields that ionize surrounding air.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 11 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">11. Dielectrics &amp; Electric Polarization</h2>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Dielectrics & Electric Polarization (<b>P</b>)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Dielectrics are non-conducting insulating substances that transmit electrical effects without conducting. Electric Polarization <b>P</b> is defined as the induced dipole moment per unit volume inside a dielectric under an external electric field: <b>P</b> = &chi;<sub>e</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <b>E</b>, where &chi;<sub>e</sub> is the electric susceptibility of the dielectric medium. SI Unit: C m<sup>-2</sup>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p><b>Mechanism of Field Reduction:</b> When a dielectric slab is placed in an external field <b>E</b><sub>0</sub>, atomic dipoles align, creating induced bound surface charges (+&sigma;<sub>p</sub> on one face and -&sigma;<sub>p</sub> on the other). These bound charges generate an internal opposing field <b>E</b><sub>p</sub> = &sigma;<sub>p</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\"><b>Net Electric Field: E = E<sub>0</sub> - E<sub>p</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E<sub>0</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">K</span>\n  </span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; K = 1 + &chi;<sub>e</sub> &gt; 1</b></div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 12 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">12. Capacitors, Capacitance &amp; Parallel Plate Capacitor (With &amp; Without Dielectric)</h2>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF512F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📌 DEFINITION: Capacitor & Capacitance (C)</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">A Capacitor is an arrangement of two conducting plates separated by an insulating dielectric medium designed to store electrostatic charge and electrical potential energy. Capacitance C is defined as the ratio of stored charge Q on either plate to the resulting potential difference V between them: C = Q / V. SI Unit: Farad (1 F = 1 C V<sup>-1</sup>). Dimensional Formula: [M<sup>-1</sup> L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>4</sup> A<sup>2</sup>].</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Working Principle of a Capacitor (Step-by-Step Mechanism)</h3>\n  <p>How does a capacitor store large charges at relatively low potential? Consider the step-wise physical mechanism:</p>\n  <ol style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li><b>Step 1 (Isolated Plate A):</b> Give positive charge +Q to an insulated metal plate A. Its potential rises to a maximum value V<sub>1</sub>. Its capacity to hold more charge is limited because adding more charge raises its potential further, causing charge leakage into surrounding air.</li>\n    <li><b>Step 2 (Bringing Uncharged Plate B Close):</b> Place an uncharged conducting plate B near plate A. Due to electrostatic induction, negative bound charge -Q is induced on the near face of B, and positive free charge +Q is induced on the far face of B. The negative charge on B attempts to reduce the potential of A, while the positive charge on B attempts to increase it. Because negative charge is closer to A, the net potential of A drops slightly.</li>\n    <li><b>Step 3 (Earthing Plate B):</b> Connect the outer face of plate B to ground (Earth). Free positive electrons flow from Earth into plate B, completely neutralizing the positive charge on the outer face. The negative charge remains tightly bound by plate A's positive field. This bound negative charge drastically lowers the potential of plate A to a new low value V' &lt;&lt; V<sub>1</sub>.</li>\n    <li><b>Conclusion:</b> Since C = Q / V', as potential V' drops dramatically for the same charge Q, the capacitance C multiplies enormously! Thus, <b>a capacitor stores large amounts of charge and energy by reducing potential through an earthed secondary plate</b>.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Derivation 1: Parallel Plate Capacitor in Vacuum\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider two parallel conducting plates P<sub>1</sub> and P<sub>2</sub> each of area A separated by small distance d in vacuum, carrying charges +Q and -Q.<br>\n  Surface charge density: &sigma; = Q / A.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Electric Field Between Plates:</b><br>\n  By Gauss's Law, field due to +plate is &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) (away) and -plate is &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) (towards).<br>\n  E<sub>0</sub> = &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) + &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = &sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A).<br><br>\n  <b>3. Potential Difference & Capacitance:</b><br>\n  Potential difference V<sub>0</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> &times; d = [ Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A) ] &times; d.<br>\n  <b>C<sub>0</sub> = \" + frac(\"Q\", \"V<sub>0</sub>\") + \" = \" + frac(\"&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A\", \"d\") + \"</b>\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 193, 7, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FFC107; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FFD54F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM FOCUS & COMMON TRAPS: Fringing Effect (Edge Effect) of Electric Field</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Near the edges of capacitor plates, electric field lines bulge outwards into curved fringing patterns due to electrostatic repulsion between like charges at boundaries. This non-uniformity is called the Fringing Effect. When plate dimensions are much larger than separation distance (d &lt;&lt; &radic;A), the fringing field at edges is negligible compared to the uniform central field, and formula C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>A/d is highly accurate.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Derivation 2: Parallel Plate Capacitor with Dielectric Slab of Thickness t (t < d)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> A dielectric slab of thickness t and dielectric constant K is placed between plates of separation d.<br>\n  - Electric field in vacuum region of thickness (d - t): E<sub>0</sub> = Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A).<br>\n  - Electric field inside dielectric slab of thickness t: E<sub>m</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> / K = Q / (K &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A).<br><br>\n  <b>2. Total Potential Difference V:</b><br>\n  V = E<sub>0</sub> (d - t) + E<sub>m</sub> t = E<sub>0</sub> (d - t) + (E<sub>0</sub> / K) t = E<sub>0</sub> [ (d - t) + (t / K) ]<br>\n  V = [ Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A) ] [ d - t + (t / K) ].<br><br>\n  <b>3. Capacitance:</b><br>\n  <b>C = \" + frac(\"Q\", \"V\") + \" = \" + frac(\"&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A\", \"d - t + (t / K)\") + \"</b><br><br>\n  <b>Special Case (Completely Filled, t = d):</b><br>\n  C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / (d/K) = <b>K (\" + frac(\"&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A\", \"d\") + \") = K C<sub>0</sub></b>. (Capacitance increases K times).\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Derivation 3: Parallel Plate Capacitor with Conducting (Metallic) Slab of Thickness t (t < d)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> A metallic conducting slab of thickness t is inserted between plates.<br>\n  Inside a conductor, electrostatic field is strictly zero: E<sub>in</sub> = 0.<br>\n  Electric field E<sub>0</sub> exists only in the air space of thickness (d - t).<br><br>\n  <b>2. Potential Difference & Capacitance:</b><br>\n  V = E<sub>0</sub> (d - t) = [ Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A) ] (d - t).<br>\n  <b>C = \" + frac(\"Q\", \"V\") + \" = \" + frac(\"&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A\", \"d - t\") + \" = C<sub>0</sub> \" + frac(\"d\", \"d - t\") + \" &gt; C<sub>0</sub></b>\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Board Exam Master Comparison Matrix: Dielectric Slab Insertion</h3>\n  <p>When a dielectric slab of constant K is inserted into a capacitor, the outcome depends critically on whether the DC charging battery remains connected or is disconnected:</p>\n\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 15px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 8px;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25); color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);\">Physical Parameter</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);\">Case A: Battery Remains CONNECTED</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);\">Case B: Battery is DISCONNECTED</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FFD700; font-weight: bold;\">1. Potential Difference (V)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #76FF03;\"><b>Remains Constant (V = V<sub>0</sub>)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FF512F;\"><b>Decreases (V' = V<sub>0</sub> / K)</b></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FFD700; font-weight: bold;\">2. Capacitance (C)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #76FF03;\"><b>Increases (C' = K C<sub>0</sub>)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #76FF03;\"><b>Increases (C' = K C<sub>0</sub>)</b></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FFD700; font-weight: bold;\">3. Stored Charge (Q)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #76FF03;\"><b>Increases (Q' = K Q<sub>0</sub>)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #00E5FF;\"><b>Remains Constant (Q = Q<sub>0</sub>)</b></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FFD700; font-weight: bold;\">4. Electric Field (E)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #76FF03;\"><b>Remains Constant (E = E<sub>0</sub>)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FF512F;\"><b>Decreases (E' = E<sub>0</sub> / K)</b></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FFD700; font-weight: bold;\">5. Stored Energy (U)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #76FF03;\"><b>Increases (U' = K U<sub>0</sub>)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: #FF512F;\"><b>Decreases (U' = U<sub>0</sub> / K)</b></td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 229, 255, 0.05); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 229, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📝 SOLVED BOARD EXAMPLE 2:</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: 500; font-size: 15.5px; margin-bottom: 10px;\">A parallel plate capacitor with air between plates has a capacitance of 8 pF. What will be the capacitance if distance between plates is reduced by half, and space between them is filled with a substance of dielectric constant K = 6?</div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3px solid #00E5FF; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00E5FF;\">Step-by-Step Solution:</b><br>Given initial capacitance C<sub>0</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d = 8 pF.<br>New separation d' = d / 2 and dielectric constant K = 6.<br>New capacitance C' = K &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d' = K &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / (d/2) = 2 K (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d) = 2 K C<sub>0</sub>.<br>C' = 2 &times; 6 &times; 8 pF = <b>96 pF</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 13 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">13. Combinations of Capacitors (Series &amp; Parallel) &amp; Energy Stored</h2>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Series Combination of Capacitors\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Connect three capacitors C<sub>1</sub>, C<sub>2</sub>, C<sub>3</sub> in series across total potential difference V.<br>\n  In series, the SAME charge Q flows through each capacitor, while applied voltage divides: V = V<sub>1</sub> + V<sub>2</sub> + V<sub>3</sub>.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Derivation:</b><br>\n  V<sub>1</sub> = Q / C<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>2</sub> = Q / C<sub>2</sub>, V<sub>3</sub> = Q / C<sub>3</sub>.<br>\n  Let equivalent capacitance be C<sub>s</sub>, so V = Q / C<sub>s</sub>.<br>\n  Q / C<sub>s</sub> = Q / C<sub>1</sub> + Q / C<sub>2</sub> + Q / C<sub>3</sub>.<br>\n  <b>1 / C<sub>s</sub> = 1 / C<sub>1</sub> + 1 / C<sub>2</sub> + 1 / C<sub>3</sub></b><br>\n  (Equivalent series capacitance is ALWAYS smaller than the smallest individual capacitor).\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Parallel Combination of Capacitors\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Setup:</b> Connect three capacitors C<sub>1</sub>, C<sub>2</sub>, C<sub>3</sub> in parallel across common potential difference V.<br>\n  Potential difference V across each capacitor is identical, while total charge divides: Q = Q<sub>1</sub> + Q<sub>2</sub> + Q<sub>3</sub>.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Derivation:</b><br>\n  Q<sub>1</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> V, Q<sub>2</sub> = C<sub>2</sub> V, Q<sub>3</sub> = C<sub>3</sub> V.<br>\n  Let equivalent capacitance be C<sub>p</sub>, so Q = C<sub>p</sub> V.<br>\n  C<sub>p</sub> V = C<sub>1</sub> V + C<sub>2</sub> V + C<sub>3</sub> V.<br>\n  <b>C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> + C<sub>3</sub></b><br>\n  (Equivalent parallel capacitance is the direct algebraic sum of individual capacitances).\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Energy Stored in a Capacitor & Energy Density\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. Energy Derivation:</b><br>\n  Let instantaneous charge be q and potential be v = q / C. Small work dW in adding charge dq is: dW = v dq = (q/C) dq.<br>\n  Total work done in charging from q = 0 to Q:<br>\n  W = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>Q</sup> (q/C) dq = (1/C) [ q<sup>2</sup> / 2 ]<sub>0</sub><sup>Q</sup> = Q<sup>2</sup> / (2C).<br>\n  <b>U = \" + frac(\"1\", \"2\") + \" C V<sup>2</sup> = \" + frac(\"Q<sup>2</sup>\", \"2 C\") + \" = \" + frac(\"1\", \"2\") + \" Q V</b><br><br>\n  <b>2. Electrostatic Energy Density (u):</b><br>\n  Substitute C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d and V = E d:<br>\n  U = 1/2 (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d) (E d)<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup> (A d) = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup> &times; Volume.<br>\n  <b>u = U / Volume = \" + frac(\"1\", \"2\") + \" &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup> [J m<sup>-3</sup>]</b>\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 22px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.4); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Loss of Electrostatic Energy on Sharing Charges between Two Capacitors\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #F1F5F9; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n      \n  <b>1. System Description:</b> Let two capacitors C<sub>1</sub> and C<sub>2</sub> charged to initial potentials V<sub>1</sub> and V<sub>2</sub> be connected in parallel.<br>\n  Initial Stored Energy: U<sub>i</sub> = 1/2 C<sub>1</sub> V<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + 1/2 C<sub>2</sub> V<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br><br>\n  <b>2. Common Potential V:</b> Total Charge / Total Capacitance = (C<sub>1</sub> V<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> V<sub>2</sub>) / (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>).<br>\n  Final Stored Energy: U<sub>f</sub> = 1/2 (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>) V<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 (C<sub>1</sub> V<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> V<sub>2</sub>)<sup>2</sup> / (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>).<br><br>\n  <b>3. Energy Loss (&Delta;U):</b><br>\n  &Delta;U = U<sub>i</sub> - U<sub>f</sub> = <b>\" + frac(\"C<sub>1</sub> C<sub>2</sub> (V<sub>1</sub> - V<sub>2</sub>)<sup>2</sup>\", \"2 (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>)\") + \" &ge; 0</b>.<br>\n  This positive energy loss is dissipated as thermal heat in connecting wires and electromagnetic radiation.\n  \n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: #0B0F19; border: 1.8px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 16px; padding: 20px; margin: 28px 0; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF512F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 16px; text-align: center; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">\n      📍 7. CAPACITORS & COMBINATIONS\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      \n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 920 520\" width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0B0F19 0%, #111827 50%, #070A10 100%);\">\n  <defs>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qRedGlow7\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#FF8A65\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <radialGradient id=\"qCyanGlow7\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"35%\" r=\"65%\">\n      <stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#38BDF8\"/>\n      <stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#0284C7\"/>\n    </radialGradient>\n    <marker id=\"mGreen7\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4.5\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L9,4.5 L0,9 Z\" fill=\"#76FF03\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(15, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">7. PARALLEL PLATE CAPACITOR (DIELECTRIC)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"65\" y=\"80\" width=\"18\" height=\"280\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#FF512F\"/>\n    <text x=\"74\" y=\"65\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">+Q (+&#963;)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"350\" y=\"80\" width=\"18\" height=\"280\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#00E5FF\"/>\n    <text x=\"359\" y=\"65\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">-Q (-&#963;)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"150\" y=\"80\" width=\"135\" height=\"280\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.18)\" stroke=\"#FFD700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dielectric (K)</text>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\">Thickness t</text>\n\n    <text x=\"165\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">-&#963;p</text>\n    <text x=\"165\" y=\"220\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">-&#963;p</text>\n    <text x=\"165\" y=\"290\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">-&#963;p</text>\n\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">+&#963;p</text>\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"220\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">+&#963;p</text>\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"290\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">+&#963;p</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"65\" y1=\"385\" x2=\"368\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"65\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"390\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"368\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"368\" y2=\"390\" stroke=\"#94A3B8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94A3B8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Distance d</text>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">C = &#949;0 A / (d - t + t/K)</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 7.1: Parallel Plate Capacitor with Dielectric</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <line x1=\"460\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"495\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,6\"/>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(470, 15)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"435\" height=\"490\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"rgba(15,23,42,0.92)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">COMBINATIONS OF CAPACITORS</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"60\" width=\"395\" height=\"175\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.03)\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"35\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Series: Q is Same, V = V1 + V2 + V3</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"110\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"125\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"117\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">C1</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"125\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"215\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"215\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"207\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">C2</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"215\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"290\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"305\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"305\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#00E5FF\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"297\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#00E5FF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">C3</text>\n    <line x1=\"305\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">1 / Cs = 1/C1 + 1/C2 + 1/C3</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"250\" width=\"395\" height=\"180\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.03)\" stroke=\"rgba(255,255,255,0.1)\"/>\n    <text x=\"35\" y=\"275\" fill=\"#FFD700\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Parallel: V is Same, Q = Q1 + Q2</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"350\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"350\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"110\" y1=\"305\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"395\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n\n    <line x1=\"110\" y1=\"315\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"315\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"190\" y1=\"295\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"335\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"295\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"335\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"197\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">C1</text>\n    <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"315\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"315\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n\n    <line x1=\"110\" y1=\"385\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <line x1=\"190\" y1=\"365\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"405\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"365\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"405\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"3.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"197\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FF512F\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">C2</text>\n    <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"385\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n\n    <line x1=\"285\" y1=\"305\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"395\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"285\" y1=\"350\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"350\" stroke=\"#FFFFFF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#76FF03\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cp = C1 + C2 + C3</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"445\" width=\"385\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(255,81,47,0.12)\" stroke=\"#FF512F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"217\" y=\"468\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#FFFFFF\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fig 7.2: Series &amp; Parallel Capacitor Circuits</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center; margin-top: 14px; line-height: 1.5; font-weight: 500;\">\n      💡 Fig 7.1: Parallel plate capacitor with dielectric slab C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>A / (d - t + t/K) & Fig 7.2: Series (1/C<sub>s</sub> = &Sigma; 1/C<sub>i</sub>) and Parallel (C<sub>p</sub> = &Sigma; C<sub>i</sub>) circuits.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- MASTER REVISION CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">⚡ Master Revision Cheat Sheet</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Summary Formulae for JKBOSE / NCERT Unit I: Electrostatics (09 Marks)</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Forces &amp; Fields:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">F = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub> / r<sup>2</sup>)<br>E<sub>axial</sub> = 2p / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>3</sup>)<br>E<sub>eq</sub> = p / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>3</sup>)<br><b>&tau;</b> = <b>p</b> &times; <b>E</b></span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Gauss's Law Applications:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">Wire: E = &lambda; / (2&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r)<br>Sheet: E = &sigma; / (2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)<br>Shell: E<sub>out</sub> = Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>2</sup>), E<sub>in</sub> = 0</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Potential &amp; Energy:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">V = Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r)<br>V<sub>dipole</sub> = p cos &theta; / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>2</sup>)<br>U = q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r)<br>U<sub>dipole</sub> = -<b>p</b> &sdot; <b>E</b></span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>• Capacitance &amp; Dielectrics:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.65; display: block;\">C<sub>0</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d, C = K C<sub>0</sub><br>1/C<sub>s</sub> = &Sigma; 1/C<sub>i</sub><br>C<sub>p</sub> = &Sigma; C<sub>i</sub><br>U = 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup> = Q<sup>2</sup> / (2C)</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c12Phy1HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <div style=\"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 25px; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF512F; padding-bottom: 10px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">Class 12 Physics Unit I: Electrostatics (09 Marks) NCERT & Master Solutions</h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #B0BEC5; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">Electric Charges, Fields, Potential & Capacitance — 100 Authentic Questions & Step-by-Step Solutions</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-left: 4px solid #FF512F; padding-left: 10px; margin-top: 25px;\">Part A: Multiple Choice Questions (Q1 - Q25)</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q1: A polythene piece rubbed with wool is found to have a negative charge of 3.2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> C. What is the number of electrons transferred from wool to polythene?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 2.0 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> electrons</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 1.6 &times; 10<sup>19</sup> electrons</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 3.2 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> electrons</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 6.25 &times; 10<sup>18</sup> electrons</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 2.0 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> electrons</span></b><br>\n      Using quantization relation q = n e: n = q / e = (3.2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> C) / (1.602 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C) = 2.0 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> electrons transferred from wool to polythene.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q2: Two point charges placed at distance d in air exert a force F. When immersed in a liquid of dielectric constant K = 4 at the same separation, what is the new electrostatic force?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) F / 4</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 4 F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 16 F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) F / 16</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) F / 4</span></b><br>\n      In a dielectric medium with dielectric constant K, electrostatic force reduces by factor K: F<sub>med</sub> = F<sub>air</sub> / K = F / 4.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q3: How does the magnitude of the electric potential V vary with distance r from an isolated point charge Q?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) V &prop; 1 / r</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) V &prop; 1 / r<sup>2</sup></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) V &prop; 1 / r<sup>3</sup></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) V is independent of r</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) V &prop; 1 / r</span></b><br>\n      Electric potential due to an isolated point charge is V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / r), which is inversely proportional to distance r (V &prop; 1 / r).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q4: The electric potential at an equatorial point of an electric dipole of moment p at distance r from center is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (p / r<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (2p / r<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (p / r<sup>3</sup>)</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Zero</span></b><br>\n      At any equatorial point, distances from +q and -q are equal. Potential V<sub>eq</sub> = V<sub>+</sub> + V<sub>-</sub> = [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> d)] - [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> d)] = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q5: What is the work done in moving a test charge q<sub>0</sub> between two points on an equipotential surface having potential V = 50 Volts?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 50 q<sub>0</sub> Joules</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) q<sub>0</sub> / 50 Joules</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 2500 q<sub>0</sub> Joules</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Zero</span></b><br>\n      Work done on an equipotential surface is W = q<sub>0</sub> (V<sub>B</sub> - V<sub>A</sub>). Since V<sub>B</sub> = V<sub>A</sub> = 50 V, &Delta;V = 0, so W = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q6: The angle between an electric field line and an equipotential surface at any point is always:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 90&deg; (Perpendicular)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 0&deg; (Parallel)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 45&deg;</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 180&deg;</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 90&deg; (Perpendicular)</span></b><br>\n      Since work done along an equipotential surface is zero (dW = <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = E dl cos &theta; = 0), cos &theta; = 0 &rArr; &theta; = 90&deg;. Electric field lines are always normal to equipotential surfaces.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q7: A parallel plate capacitor with air between plates has capacitance C<sub>0</sub> = 10 &mu;F. If a dielectric slab of dielectric constant K = 6 completely fills the space between plates, the new capacitance is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 60 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 1.67 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 10 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 360 &mu;F</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 60 &mu;F</span></b><br>\n      When a dielectric slab of constant K completely fills the gap, capacitance increases by factor K: C = K C<sub>0</sub> = 6 &times; 10 &mu;F = 60 &mu;F.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q8: Three capacitors of capacitances 2 &mu;F, 3 &mu;F, and 6 &mu;F are connected in series. The equivalent capacitance of the combination is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 1.0 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 11 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 0.5 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 6.0 &mu;F</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 1.0 &mu;F</span></b><br>\n      In series: 1 / C<sub>s</sub> = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = (3 + 2 + 1) / 6 = 6 / 6 = 1 &mu;F<sup>-1</sup> &rArr; C<sub>s</sub> = 1.0 &mu;F.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q9: Three capacitors of capacitances 2 &mu;F, 3 &mu;F, and 4 &mu;F are connected in parallel. What is the total capacitance of the combination?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 9 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 0.92 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 24 &mu;F</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 1.5 &mu;F</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 9 &mu;F</span></b><br>\n      In parallel: C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> + C<sub>3</sub> = 2 + 3 + 4 = 9 &mu;F.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q10: The electrostatic energy stored in a capacitor of capacitance C charged to potential difference V is given by:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) C V</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 1/2 C<sup>2</sup> V</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 2 C V<sup>2</sup></div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup></span></b><br>\n      Work done in charging a capacitor is stored as potential energy U = &int; V dq = &int; (q/C) dq = q<sup>2</sup> / (2C) = 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q11: For a short electric dipole of moment p, the electric field intensity at an axial point at distance r varies as:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 1 / r<sup>3</sup></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 1 / r<sup>2</sup></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 1 / r</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) r<sup>3</sup></div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 1 / r<sup>3</sup></span></b><br>\n      Axial field of a short dipole is E<sub>axial</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (2p / r<sup>3</sup>) &prop; 1 / r<sup>3</sup>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q12: An electric dipole of dipole moment <b>p</b> is placed in a uniform electric field <b>E</b>. The torque acting on the dipole is maximum when the angle &theta; between <b>p</b> and <b>E</b> is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 90&deg;</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 0&deg;</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 180&deg;</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 45&deg;</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 90&deg;</span></b><br>\n      Torque magnitude is &tau; = p E sin &theta;. Maximum torque occurs when sin &theta; = 1, i.e., &theta; = 90&deg; (&tau;<sub>max</sub> = p E).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q13: A Gaussian sphere encloses an electric dipole of moment p. What is the total electric flux emerging through the spherical surface?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) p / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 2p / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Infinity</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Zero</span></b><br>\n      A dipole consists of equal and opposite charges (+q and -q). Net enclosed charge q<sub>enclosed</sub> = +q - q = 0. By Gauss's Law, &Phi;<sub>E</sub> = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q14: A thin metallic spherical shell of radius R carries uniform surface charge Q. The electric field intensity at distance r < R (inside the shell) is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / r<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / R<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Q / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Zero</span></b><br>\n      A Gaussian surface inside the hollow metallic shell encloses zero net charge (q<sub>enclosed</sub> = 0). By Gauss's Law, E<sub>in</sub> = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q15: What is the electric potential inside a charged hollow spherical conductor of radius R carrying charge Q?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Constant and equal to [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / R)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) Inversely proportional to r</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Directly proportional to r</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Constant and equal to [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / R)</span></b><br>\n      Since E = -dV/dr = 0 inside a charged conductor, potential V is constant everywhere throughout the interior volume and equals the surface potential V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / R).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q16: If the distance between the two plates of a parallel plate capacitor is halved and plate area is doubled, its capacitance will:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Increase 4 times</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) Increase 2 times</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) Remain unchanged</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Decrease 4 times</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Increase 4 times</span></b><br>\n      Capacitance C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d. When A' = 2A and d' = d/2: C' = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (2A) / (d/2) = 4 (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d) = 4 C.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q17: A capacitor is charged by a battery and then disconnected. A dielectric slab is now inserted between plates. What happens to the stored charge and potential difference?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Charge Q remains constant, Potential difference V decreases by factor K</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) Charge increases, V remains constant</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) Both Q and V increase</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Both Q and V decrease</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Charge Q remains constant, Potential difference V decreases by factor K</span></b><br>\n      Since the battery is disconnected, charge is conserved (Q = constant). Capacitance increases to C' = K C. Therefore, potential difference decreases: V' = Q / C' = V / K.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q18: The electric field intensity produced by an infinitely large thin plane sheet with uniform surface charge density &sigma; is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) &sigma; / (2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) (independent of distance)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) &sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) &sigma; / (2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) &sigma; / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) &sigma; / (2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) (independent of distance)</span></b><br>\n      By Gauss's Law, flux through a cylindrical pillbox is 2 E A = &sigma; A / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; E = &sigma; / (2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>), independent of distance r.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q19: What is the potential energy of an electric dipole of moment <b>p</b> in a uniform electric field <b>E</b> when aligned at angle &theta; = 180&deg; (unstable equilibrium)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) +p E (Maximum)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) -p E (Minimum)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 2 p E</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) +p E (Maximum)</span></b><br>\n      Potential energy U = -p E cos &theta;. At &theta; = 180&deg;, cos 180&deg; = -1 &rArr; U = -p E (-1) = +p E (maximum potential energy).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q20: What is the value of the dielectric constant (relative permittivity) for a perfect metallic conductor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Infinity (&infin;)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 1</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 80</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Infinity (&infin;)</span></b><br>\n      Inside a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium, induced charges completely cancel the external field (E<sub>net</sub> = 0). Since K = E<sub>0</sub> / E<sub>net</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> / 0 = &infin;.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q21: Two point charges +q and -q are located at (0, 0, -a) and (0, 0, a). What is the electric potential at the point (x, y, 0) on the xy-plane?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Zero</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> a)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 2q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> a)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Inversely proportional to &radic;(x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>)</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Zero</span></b><br>\n      The xy-plane (z = 0) is the perpendicular bisector (equatorial plane) of the dipole. Distance from (x, y, 0) to (0, 0, a) and (0, 0, -a) is identical d = &radic;(x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>). Hence V = V<sub>+</sub> + V<sub>-</sub> = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q22: The electric potential in a region of space is given by V(x, y, z) = 6x - 8xy - 8y + 6yz. What is the electric force experienced by a charge of 2 C at origin (0, 0, 0)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 20 N</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 10 N</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 2 N</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Zero</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 20 N</span></b><br>\n      E<sub>x</sub> = -&part;V/&part;x = -(6 - 8y) = -6 at (0,0,0); E<sub>y</sub> = -&part;V/&part;y = -(-8x - 8 + 6z) = +8; E<sub>z</sub> = -&part;V/&part;z = -6y = 0. E<sub>net</sub> = &radic;((-6)<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup>) = 10 N C<sup>-1</sup>. Force F = q E = 2 &times; 10 = 20 N.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q23: A capacitor of 20 &mu;F is charged to 500 V. The energy stored in the capacitor is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) 2.5 Joules</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) 5.0 Joules</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) 10 Joules</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) 0.05 Joules</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) 2.5 Joules</span></b><br>\n      U = 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &times; (20 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> F) &times; (500 V)<sup>2</sup> = 10 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> &times; 250,000 = 2.5 Joules.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q24: When two identical charged conducting spheres carrying potentials V<sub>1</sub> and V<sub>2</sub> are connected by a thin wire, the common potential V is:</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) (V<sub>1</sub> + V<sub>2</sub>) / 2</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) V<sub>1</sub> + V<sub>2</sub></div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) &radic;(V<sub>1</sub> V<sub>2</sub>)</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) (V<sub>1</sub> - V<sub>2</sub>) / 2</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) (V<sub>1</sub> + V<sub>2</sub>) / 2</span></b><br>\n      For two identical spheres C<sub>1</sub> = C<sub>2</sub> = C. Common potential V = Total charge / Total capacitance = (C V<sub>1</sub> + C V<sub>2</sub>) / (2C) = (V<sub>1</sub> + V<sub>2</sub>) / 2.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q25: Which of the following physical quantities is a vector quantity?</h3>\n    <div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: left !important; line-height: 1.8; color: #FFCC80;\">\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">a) Electric Dipole Moment</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">b) Electric Potential</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">c) Electric Flux</div>\n      <div class=\"mcq-option\" style=\"text-align: left !important; margin: 4px 0;\">d) Electrostatic Potential Energy</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>Correct Answer: <span style=\"color: #FF512F;\">a) Electric Dipole Moment</span></b><br>\n      Electric dipole moment <b>p</b> = q (2<b>a</b>) is a vector directed from -q to +q. Electric potential, electric flux, and electrostatic potential energy are all scalar quantities.\n    </div>\n  </div><h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-left: 4px solid #FF512F; padding-left: 10px; margin-top: 30px;\">Part B: Very Short Answer Questions (Q26 - Q55)</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q26: Define one Coulomb (1 C) of electric charge using Coulomb's Law.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      One Coulomb is defined as that quantity of electrostatic charge which, when placed in vacuum at a distance of 1 meter from an equal and similar charge, repels it with an electrostatic force of 9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Newtons.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q27: State the Principle of Quantization of electric charge and give its mathematical equation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      The Principle of Quantization of Charge states that the total electric charge possessed by any physical body is always an integral multiple of a basic elementary quantum of charge e: <b>q = &plusmn;n e</b>, where n = 1, 2, 3, ... and e = 1.602 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q28: Define Electric Potential at a point and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Electric Potential V at a point in an electric field is defined as the amount of work done per unit positive test charge by an external agent in bringing it from infinity to that point against electrostatic forces: <b>V = W / q<sub>0</sub></b>. Its SI unit is Volt (V = J C<sup>-1</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q29: Define Electric Potential Difference between two points in an electrostatic field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Electric Potential Difference &Delta;V between two points A and B is defined as the work done in carrying a unit positive test charge from point A to point B against electrostatic field forces: <b>V<sub>B</sub> - V<sub>A</sub> = W<sub>A&rarr;B</sub> / q<sub>0</sub></b> [Volt V].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q30: Why can two electric field lines never intersect each other at any point?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      If two electric field lines intersected at a point, two tangents could be drawn at the point of intersection, indicating two different directions of resultant electric field at that single location, which is physically impossible.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q31: Define an Equipotential Surface and give one example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      An Equipotential Surface is a surface on which the electrostatic potential has the same constant value at every point. Example: Concentric spherical surfaces surrounding an isolated point charge.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q32: Why is the work done in moving a charge between two points on an equipotential surface zero?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Work done is given by W = q<sub>0</sub> (V<sub>B</sub> - V<sub>A</sub>). Since the potential at all points on an equipotential surface is identical (V<sub>B</sub> = V<sub>A</sub>), potential difference &Delta;V = 0, hence W = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q33: Write the relation between electric field intensity E and electric potential gradient dV/dr.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>E = - (dV / dr)</b>. This shows that the electric field intensity equals the negative spatial gradient of electric potential, and electric field lines always point in the direction of steepest decrease in potential.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q34: Define Capacitance of a conductor and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Capacitance C of a conductor is defined as the ratio of electrostatic charge Q given to the conductor to the resulting rise in its electrical potential V: <b>C = Q / V</b>. Its SI unit is Farad (F = C V<sup>-1</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q35: Define one Farad (1 F) of capacitance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      A conductor has a capacitance of one Farad if an electric charge of one Coulomb raises its electrical potential by one Volt: <b>1 Farad = 1 Coulomb / 1 Volt (1 F = 1 C V<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q36: What is the physical meaning of Dielectric Polarization?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Dielectric Polarization is the alignment of molecular dipole moments (or displacement of positive and negative charges) within an insulating material under the influence of an applied external electric field, creating an induced dipole moment per unit volume <b>P</b> = &chi;<sub>e</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <b>E</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q37: Define Electric Dipole Moment vector and state its SI unit and direction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      The Electric Dipole Moment vector <b>p</b> is defined as the product of charge magnitude q and separation vector 2<b>a</b>: <b>p = q (2a)</b>. Its SI unit is Coulomb-meter (C m), and it is directed from negative charge (-q) to positive charge (+q).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q38: What is Electrostatic Shielding?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Electrostatic shielding is the phenomenon of protecting a sensitive spatial region or electrical instrument from external electrostatic fields by enclosing it inside a hollow conductor, based on the fact that electric field inside a metallic cavity is strictly zero (E<sub>in</sub> = 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q39: State Gauss's Theorem in electrostatics.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Gauss's Theorem states that the total outward electric flux &Phi;<sub>E</sub> passing through any closed three-dimensional Gaussian surface in free space equals 1/&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> times the net algebraic electric charge enclosed inside the surface: <b>&oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q40: Write the expression for electrostatic potential energy of two point charges q<sub>1</sub> and q<sub>2</sub> separated by distance r in vacuum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>U = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub> / r]</b> [Joules J].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q41: State the formula for the electric potential on the axial line of an electric dipole of moment p at distance r.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>V<sub>axial</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [p / r<sup>2</sup>]</b> (for a short dipole r >> a).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q42: What is the shape of equipotential surfaces for a uniform electric field directed along the z-axis?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Equipotential surfaces for a uniform field along the z-axis are a series of <b>equidistant planes parallel to the xy-plane</b> (perpendicular to the z-axis).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q43: What is the formula for the capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor in vacuum?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>C<sub>0</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d</b>, where A is the area of each plate and d is the distance between them.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q44: How does the capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor change when a dielectric slab of dielectric constant K is introduced between its plates?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      The capacitance increases by a factor of K: <b>C = K C<sub>0</sub> = K (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q45: State the formula for equivalent capacitance of two capacitors C<sub>1</sub> and C<sub>2</sub> connected in series.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>1 / C<sub>s</sub> = 1 / C<sub>1</sub> + 1 / C<sub>2</sub> &rArr; C<sub>s</sub> = (C<sub>1</sub> C<sub>2</sub>) / (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q46: State the formula for equivalent capacitance of two capacitors C<sub>1</sub> and C<sub>2</sub> connected in parallel.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q47: Write the formula for electrostatic energy density in the electric field between capacitor plates.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Electrostatic Energy Density <b>u = (1/2) &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup></b> [Joules per cubic meter J m<sup>-3</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q48: Why does the electrostatic field inside a conductor vanish under static equilibrium?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      In an electrostatic field, free electrons in a conductor redistribute themselves across the outer boundary until the induced internal electric field exactly equals and cancels the external applied field, making net internal field E = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q49: What is the value of electric potential at the surface and inside a charged spherical conductor of radius R carrying charge Q?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Both at the surface and everywhere inside the spherical conductor, the electric potential is uniform and constant: <b>V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / R)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q50: Can electric potential be zero at a point where electric field is non-zero? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Yes. At any point on the equatorial line of an electric dipole, the electric potential is strictly zero (V = 0), while the electric field intensity is non-zero (E = p / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>3</sup>)).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q51: Can electric field be zero at a point where electric potential is non-zero? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Yes. Inside a uniformly charged hollow spherical shell, the electric field is strictly zero (E = 0), but the electric potential is non-zero and constant (V = Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> R)).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q52: What is the dielectric strength of a medium?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Dielectric strength is defined as the maximum electric field intensity that an insulating material can withstand without electrical breakdown (ionization and conduction). For dry air, it is approximately 3 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q53: What is the net charge on a charged capacitor carrying charges +Q and -Q on its two plates?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      The net algebraic charge on a capacitor as a whole is strictly zero: <b>Q<sub>net</sub> = +Q + (-Q) = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q54: State the condition under which the potential energy of an electric dipole in a uniform electric field is minimum (stable equilibrium).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      Potential energy U = -p E cos &theta; is minimum (U = -p E) when the dipole moment <b>p</b> is aligned parallel to the uniform electric field <b>E</b> (&theta; = 0&deg;).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q55: State the formula for electric field at an axial point of a short dipole.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Explanation:</b>\n      <b>E<sub>axial</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [2p / r<sup>3</sup>]</b>, directed parallel to dipole moment vector <b>p</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div><h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-left: 4px solid #FF512F; padding-left: 10px; margin-top: 30px;\">Part C: Short Answer Questions (Q56 - Q85)</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q56: Calculate the electric potential at a point P located 9.0 cm away from an isolated point charge of +4.0 &mu;C in vacuum. Hence, find the work done in bringing a test charge of 2.0 &times; 10<sup>-9</sup> C from infinity to point P.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      1. <b>Electric Potential at P:</b><br>Given: Q = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> C, r = 9.0 cm = 0.09 m, 1/(4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = 9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-2</sup>.<br>V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / r) = (9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup>) &times; (4.0 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) / 0.09 = <b>4.0 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> Volts</b>.<br><br>2. <b>Work Done:</b><br>W = q<sub>0</sub> V = (2.0 &times; 10<sup>-9</sup> C) &times; (4.0 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> V) = <b>8.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> Joules</b>.<br>This work is independent of the path taken by the charge.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q57: Derive the relation E = - dV/dr between electric field intensity and electric potential gradient.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Consider two closely spaced equipotential surfaces A and B separated by small perpendicular distance dr, with potentials V and (V - dV).<br><br>Work done dW in moving unit positive test charge (+1 C) from surface B to A against electric field <b>E</b> is:<br>dW = Force &times; Displacement = E &times; dr.<br><br>By definition of potential difference, work done equals potential difference:<br>dW = V<sub>A</sub> - V<sub>B</sub> = V - (V - dV) = dV.<br><br>Equating both expressions for work done:<br>E &times; (-dr) = dV &rArr; <b>E = - (dV / dr)</b>.<br>The negative sign indicates that the direction of electric field is in the direction of decreasing potential.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q58: Three point charges +q, +2q, and -3q are placed at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side length L. Find the total electrostatic potential energy of the system.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Total electrostatic potential energy U is the sum of interaction energies of all charge pairs:<br>U = U<sub>12</sub> + U<sub>23</sub> + U<sub>31</sub><br>U = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> L)] [ (q)(2q) + (2q)(-3q) + (-3q)(q) ]<br>U = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> L)] [ 2q<sup>2</sup> - 6q<sup>2</sup> - 3q<sup>2</sup> ]<br><b>U = - [7 q<sup>2</sup> / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> L)]</b> [Joules].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q59: A parallel plate capacitor with plate area A = 100 cm<sup>2</sup> and plate separation d = 2.0 mm is connected to a 100 V DC supply. Calculate: (i) the capacitance, (ii) the charge on each plate, and (iii) the electrostatic energy stored.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Given: A = 100 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> m<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>-2</sup> m<sup>2</sup>, d = 2.0 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> m, V = 100 V, &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = 8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> F m<sup>-1</sup>.<br><br>1. <b>Capacitance:</b><br>C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d = (8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> &times; 10<sup>-2</sup>) / (2.0 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) = <b>44.27 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> F = 44.27 pF</b>.<br><br>2. <b>Charge on Plates:</b><br>Q = C V = (44.27 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> F) &times; 100 V = <b>4.427 &times; 10<sup>-9</sup> C = 4.427 nC</b>.<br><br>3. <b>Energy Stored:</b><br>U = 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &times; (44.27 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (100)<sup>2</sup> = <b>2.2135 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> Joules</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q60: Two capacitors of capacitances 6 &mu;F and 12 &mu;F are connected in series across a 120 V battery. Find the potential difference and charge across each capacitor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      1. <b>Equivalent Series Capacitance:</b><br>1 / C<sub>s</sub> = 1/6 + 1/12 = 3/12 = 1/4 &rArr; C<sub>s</sub> = 4 &mu;F.<br><br>2. <b>Total Charge Supplied by Battery:</b><br>Q = C<sub>s</sub> V = 4 &mu;F &times; 120 V = <b>480 &mu;C</b>.<br>In series, charge on both capacitors is identical: Q<sub>1</sub> = Q<sub>2</sub> = 480 &mu;C.<br><br>3. <b>Potential Difference Across Each:</b><br>V<sub>1</sub> = Q / C<sub>1</sub> = 480 &mu;C / 6 &mu;F = <b>80 Volts</b>.<br>V<sub>2</sub> = Q / C<sub>2</sub> = 480 &mu;C / 12 &mu;F = <b>40 Volts</b>.<br>(Check: V<sub>1</sub> + V<sub>2</sub> = 80 + 40 = 120 V).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q61: Derive an expression for the equivalent capacitance of three capacitors C<sub>1</sub>, C<sub>2</sub>, and C<sub>3</sub> connected in series.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      In a series combination, the same charge Q flows onto each capacitor, while the total applied voltage V divides across them:<br>V = V<sub>1</sub> + V<sub>2</sub> + V<sub>3</sub>.<br><br>Using V = Q / C:<br>V<sub>1</sub> = Q / C<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>2</sub> = Q / C<sub>2</sub>, V<sub>3</sub> = Q / C<sub>3</sub>.<br><br>Let C<sub>s</sub> be the equivalent capacitance, so V = Q / C<sub>s</sub>.<br>Q / C<sub>s</sub> = Q / C<sub>1</sub> + Q / C<sub>2</sub> + Q / C<sub>3</sub>.<br><br>Dividing by Q on both sides:<br><b>1 / C<sub>s</sub> = 1 / C<sub>1</sub> + 1 / C<sub>2</sub> + 1 / C<sub>3</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q62: Derive an expression for the equivalent capacitance of three capacitors C<sub>1</sub>, C<sub>2</sub>, and C<sub>3</sub> connected in parallel.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      In a parallel combination, the potential difference V across each capacitor is identical, while the total charge Q drawn from the battery is distributed among them:<br>Q = Q<sub>1</sub> + Q<sub>2</sub> + Q<sub>3</sub>.<br><br>Using Q = C V:<br>Q<sub>1</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> V, Q<sub>2</sub> = C<sub>2</sub> V, Q<sub>3</sub> = C<sub>3</sub> V.<br><br>Let C<sub>p</sub> be the equivalent parallel capacitance, so Q = C<sub>p</sub> V.<br>C<sub>p</sub> V = C<sub>1</sub> V + C<sub>2</sub> V + C<sub>3</sub> V.<br><br>Dividing by V on both sides:<br><b>C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> + C<sub>3</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q63: Explain why the electric field inside a dielectric slab decreases when placed in an external uniform electric field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      When a dielectric slab is subjected to an external electric field <b>E</b><sub>0</sub>, electric polarization induces bound surface charges of opposite signs on the opposite faces of the slab.<br>These induced charges set up an internal opposing electric field <b>E</b><sub>p</sub> inside the dielectric.<br>The resultant net electric field inside the dielectric is the vector difference:<br><b>E = E<sub>0</sub> - E<sub>p</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> / K</b>, where K &gt; 1 is the dielectric constant.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q64: Two point charges of +2 &mu;C and -2 &mu;C are separated by a distance of 20 cm in air. Calculate the electric potential and electric field at the midpoint of the line joining them.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Let midpoint be M. Distance of M from each charge is r = 10 cm = 0.10 m.<br><br>1. <b>Electric Potential at Midpoint:</b><br>V = V<sub>+</sub> + V<sub>-</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ (2 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> / 0.10) + (-2 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> / 0.10) ] = <b>0 Volts</b>.<br><br>2. <b>Electric Field at Midpoint:</b><br>Fields due to +2 &mu;C (away from it) and -2 &mu;C (towards it) point in the SAME direction (towards the negative charge):<br>E<sub>net</sub> = E<sub>+</sub> + E<sub>-</sub> = 2 &times; [ (9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup>) &times; (2 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) / (0.10)<sup>2</sup> ]<br>E<sub>net</sub> = 2 &times; [ 18 &times; 10<sup>3</sup> / 0.01 ] = <b>3.6 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> N C<sup>-1</sup> (directed towards -2 &mu;C charge)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q65: State four essential properties of equipotential surfaces.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      1. <b>Zero Work Done:</b> Work done in moving any charge between two points on an equipotential surface is zero (W = 0).<br>2. <b>Normal Field Lines:</b> Electric field lines are always perpendicular (normal) to the equipotential surface at every point.<br>3. <b>Non-Intersecting:</b> Two equipotential surfaces can never intersect each other.<br>4. <b>Spacing and Field Strength:</b> Closer spacing of equipotential surfaces indicates regions of stronger electric field intensity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q66: A 900 pF capacitor is charged by a 100 V battery. How much electrostatic energy is stored by the capacitor? It is then disconnected from the battery and connected to another uncharged 900 pF capacitor. What is the electrostatic energy stored by the system?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      1. <b>Initial Energy:</b><br>U<sub>i</sub> = 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &times; (900 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> F) &times; (100 V)<sup>2</sup> = <b>4.5 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> Joules</b>.<br><br>2. <b>Connected to Uncharged Capacitor:</b><br>Common potential V' = Q / (2C) = (C V) / (2C) = V / 2 = 50 V.<br>Total equivalent capacitance C<sub>total</sub> = 2C = 1800 pF.<br>Final Energy U<sub>f</sub> = 1/2 C<sub>total</sub> (V')<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &times; (1800 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (50)<sup>2</sup> = <b>2.25 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> Joules</b>.<br><br>Energy lost in redistribution (as heat and electromagnetic radiation) = U<sub>i</sub> - U<sub>f</sub> = 2.25 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> J.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q67: Derive an expression for the torque acting on an electric dipole placed in a uniform electric field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Consider a dipole with charges +q and -q separated by 2a in a uniform field <b>E</b> at angle &theta;.<br>Force on +q: <b>F</b><sub>+</sub> = +q <b>E</b>.<br>Force on -q: <b>F</b><sub>-</sub> = -q <b>E</b>.<br>Net translational force = 0.<br>Perpendicular distance between the two lines of action = 2a sin &theta;.<br>Torque &tau; = Force &times; Perpendicular distance = (q E) &times; (2a sin &theta;) = (q 2a) E sin &theta;.<br>Since p = q (2a): <b>&tau; = p E sin &theta;</b> (Vectorially: <b>&tau; = p &times; E</b>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q68: Explain the concept of Electrostatic Shielding and write two practical applications.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Electrostatic shielding is the phenomenon of protecting a region of space from external electric fields by enclosing it within a hollow conducting enclosure (since E = 0 inside a cavity).<br><br><b>Practical Applications:</b><br>1. <b>Sensitive Instruments:</b> Enclosing sensitive electronic meters inside metallic Faraday cages to prevent interference from external electric fields.<br>2. <b>Thunderstorm Protection:</b> Staying inside a closed metallic car during lightning, as the metal body conducts lightning around the occupants into the ground.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q69: A spherical conductor of radius 12 cm has a charge of 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> C distributed uniformly on its surface. What is the electric field: (i) inside the sphere, (ii) just outside the sphere, and (iii) at a point 18 cm from the center?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Given: R = 0.12 m, Q = 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> C.<br><br>1. <b>Inside the sphere (r < R):</b> <b>E = 0</b>.<br>2. <b>Just outside the sphere (r = R = 0.12 m):</b><br>E = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / R<sup>2</sup>) = (9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) / (0.12)<sup>2</sup> = 1440 / 0.0144 = <b>1.0 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> N C<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>3. <b>At r = 0.18 m:</b><br>E = (9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) / (0.18)<sup>2</sup> = 1440 / 0.0324 = <b>4.44 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> N C<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q70: Derive an expression for the work done in rotating an electric dipole from angle &theta;<sub>1</sub> to &theta;<sub>2</sub> in a uniform electric field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Work done dW in rotating dipole through d&theta; against restoring torque is dW = &tau; d&theta; = p E sin &theta; d&theta;.<br><br>Total work done from &theta;<sub>1</sub> to &theta;<sub>2</sub>:<br>W = &int;<sub>&theta;<sub>1</sub></sub><sup>&theta;<sub>2</sub></sup> p E sin &theta; d&theta; = p E [-cos &theta;]<sub>&theta;<sub>1</sub></sub><sup>&theta;<sub>2</sub></sup><br><b>W = p E (cos &theta;<sub>1</sub> - cos &theta;<sub>2</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q71: Calculate the equivalent capacitance between terminals A and B of a bridge network containing five capacitors each of capacitance C = 10 &mu;F.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      The 5 identical capacitors form a balanced Wheatstone bridge network (C<sub>1</sub> / C<sub>2</sub> = C<sub>3</sub> / C<sub>4</sub> = 1).<br>Since the bridge is balanced, the potential difference across the central bridge capacitor is zero, so no charge flows through it and it can be removed from the circuit.<br><br>The circuit simplifies to two parallel branches, each having two capacitors in series:<br>- Branch 1: C<sub>s1</sub> = (C &times; C) / (C + C) = C / 2 = 5 &mu;F.<br>- Branch 2: C<sub>s2</sub> = (C &times; C) / (C + C) = C / 2 = 5 &mu;F.<br><br>Equivalent capacitance C<sub>AB</sub> = C<sub>s1</sub> + C<sub>s2</sub> = 5 + 5 = <b>10 &mu;F = C</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q72: Distinguish between Polar and Non-Polar Dielectrics with two examples each.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      1. <b>Non-Polar Dielectrics:</b> The centers of positive and negative charges coincide in the absence of an external electric field, so molecules have zero permanent dipole moment. Examples: H<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>.<br>2. <b>Polar Dielectrics:</b> The centers of positive and negative charges do not coincide due to asymmetric molecular shape, possessing permanent dipole moments even without an external field. Examples: H<sub>2</sub>O, HCl, NH<sub>3</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q73: An infinite line charge produces an electric field of magnitude 9.0 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> N C<sup>-1</sup> at a distance of 2.0 cm. Calculate the linear charge density &lambda;.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Given: E = 9.0 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> N C<sup>-1</sup>, r = 0.02 m.<br>E = &lambda; / (2&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r) = [2 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (&lambda; / r)<br>9.0 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> = (2 &times; 9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; &lambda;) / 0.02 = (9 &times; 10<sup>11</sup>) &lambda;<br><b>&lambda; = (9.0 &times; 10<sup>4</sup>) / (9.0 &times; 10<sup>11</sup>) = 1.0 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> C m<sup>-1</sup> = 0.1 &mu;C m<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q74: What is the electrostatic potential energy of a dipole of moment 5 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> C m in a uniform field of 10<sup>4</sup> N C<sup>-1</sup> at: (i) stable equilibrium, and (ii) unstable equilibrium?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      1. <b>Stable Equilibrium (&theta; = 0&deg;):</b><br>U = -p E cos 0&deg; = -(5 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup>) &times; (10<sup>4</sup>) &times; 1 = <b>-5.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> Joules</b>.<br><br>2. <b>Unstable Equilibrium (&theta; = 180&deg;):</b><br>U = -p E cos 180&deg; = -(5 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup>) &times; (10<sup>4</sup>) &times; (-1) = <b>+5.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> Joules</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q75: A cube of side L has a charge q placed at its center. What is the electric flux through each face of the cube?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      By Gauss's Law, the total outward flux through the entire closed cube is &Phi;<sub>total</sub> = q / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.<br>By cubic symmetry, flux is equally distributed across all 6 identical faces.<br>Flux through one face = <b>&Phi;<sub>one face</sub> = q / (6 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q76: Show that the electric field at any point between two oppositely charged infinite parallel plane sheets of surface charge densities +&sigma; and -&sigma; is &sigma;/&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>, while outside it is zero.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      1. <b>Between the sheets:</b> Electric field due to positive sheet (E<sub>+</sub> = &sigma;/2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>, away) and negative sheet (E<sub>-</sub> = &sigma;/2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>, towards) point in the SAME direction:<br><b>E<sub>inside</sub> = &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) + &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = &sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></b>.<br><br>2. <b>Outside the sheets:</b> Fields are equal in magnitude and OPPOSITE in direction:<br><b>E<sub>outside</sub> = &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) - &sigma;/(2&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q77: Explain why the capacitance of a capacitor increases when a conducting slab of thickness t (t < d) is introduced between its plates.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      When a conducting slab of thickness t is inserted between capacitor plates of separation d, the electric field inside the conductor is zero (E = 0).<br>Electric field E<sub>0</sub> now exists only over distance (d - t).<br>Potential difference reduces to: V = E<sub>0</sub> (d - t) = (Q / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A) (d - t).<br>Capacitance becomes: <b>C = Q / V = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / (d - t)</b>.<br>Since (d - t) &lt; d, the capacitance C is strictly greater than C<sub>0</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q78: Calculate the total number of electrons constituting 1 Coulomb of negative charge.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Using quantization formula q = n e:<br>n = q / e = (1.0 C) / (1.602 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C) = <b>6.242 &times; 10<sup>18</sup> electrons</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q79: Calculate the electrostatic force between two protons separated by 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-15</sup> m inside an atomic nucleus.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      F = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (e<sup>2</sup> / r<sup>2</sup>)<br>F = (9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup>) &times; (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>)<sup>2</sup> / (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-15</sup>)<sup>2</sup><br>F = (9 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> &times; 2.56 &times; 10<sup>-38</sup>) / (2.56 &times; 10<sup>-30</sup>) = <b>90 Newtons (Repulsive)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q80: What is the ratio of electric field at an axial point to an equatorial point of a short dipole at the same distance r?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Axial field: E<sub>axial</sub> = 2p / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>3</sup>). Equatorial field: E<sub>eq</sub> = p / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r<sup>3</sup>).<br>Ratio: <b>E<sub>axial</sub> / E<sub>eq</sub> = 2 : 1</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q81: Define Electric Flux (&Phi;<sub>E</sub>) and state its SI unit and dimensional formula.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Electric Flux &Phi;<sub>E</sub> is the measure of the total number of electric field lines passing normally through a given area: &Phi;<sub>E</sub> = &int;&int; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b>.<br>SI Unit: <b>N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-1</sup> or Volt meter (V m)</b>.<br>Dimensional Formula: <b>[M L<sup>3</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q82: A point charge q is placed at one corner of a cube of edge a. Calculate the electric flux passing through the cube.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      To enclose the corner charge symmetrically, 8 identical cubes must be assembled sharing that corner vertex.<br>Total flux through all 8 cubes is q / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.<br>Therefore, flux through one single cube is: <b>&Phi; = q / (8 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q83: Two metallic spheres of radii r<sub>1</sub> and r<sub>2</sub> are charged to the same potential V. Find the ratio of their surface charge densities &sigma;<sub>1</sub> / &sigma;<sub>2</sub>.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Potential of a sphere V = Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r) = (&sigma; 4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>) / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r) = &sigma; r / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.<br>Since V is identical: &sigma;<sub>1</sub> r<sub>1</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = &sigma;<sub>2</sub> r<sub>2</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <b>&sigma;<sub>1</sub> / &sigma;<sub>2</sub> = r<sub>2</sub> / r<sub>1</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q84: Why does the capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor increase when plate area A is increased?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Capacitance is C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d. Increasing the plate area A allows the capacitor to hold more electric charge Q at the same electric field and surface charge density &sigma; = Q/A, thereby reducing potential difference V for a given charge and increasing capacitance.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q85: Calculate the energy density in a region of space where a uniform electric field of magnitude 10<sup>5</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup> exists in vacuum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</b>\n      Energy density u = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &times; (8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> F m<sup>-1</sup>) &times; (10<sup>5</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup>)<sup>2</sup><br>u = 0.5 &times; (8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (10<sup>10</sup>) = <b>0.04427 J m<sup>-3</sup> = 4.427 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> J m<sup>-3</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div><h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; border-left: 4px solid #FF512F; padding-left: 10px; margin-top: 30px;\">Part D: Long Answer Questions (Q86 - Q100)</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q86: Derive an expression for the electric potential at a point at distance r from an isolated positive point charge Q in vacuum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Theoretical Setup:</b><br>Consider an isolated point charge +Q placed at origin O in vacuum. We wish to determine electric potential V at point P located at distance r from O.<br>By definition, V equals work done in moving unit positive test charge (+1 C) from infinity to P against repulsive field of +Q.<br><br><b>2. Force at Intermediate Point:</b><br>Let the test charge be at an intermediate point A at distance x from O. Electrostatic force on +1 C is:<br>F = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q &times; 1 / x<sup>2</sup>) = [Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> x<sup>2</sup>)] (directed radially away along OA).<br><br><b>3. Work Done for Small Displacement:</b><br>Small work done dW in moving test charge against force F through displacement dx towards O is:<br>dW = <b>F</b> &sdot; d<b>x</b> = F dx cos 180&deg; = -F dx = - [Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> x<sup>2</sup>)] dx.<br><br><b>4. Integration from &infin; to r:</b><br>Total work done W = &int;<sub>&infin;</sub><sup>r</sup> - [Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> x<sup>2</sup>)] dx = - [Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] &int;<sub>&infin;</sub><sup>r</sup> x<sup>-2</sup> dx<br>W = - [Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ -1/x ]<sub>&infin;</sub><sup>r</sup> = [Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ 1/r - 1/&infin; ]<br><br><b>V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [Q / r]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q87: Derive an expression for the electric potential at any general point (r, &theta;) due to a short electric dipole of moment p. Hence, deduce expressions for axial and equatorial points.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Geometry and Setup:</b><br>Consider a dipole consisting of charges -q at A and +q at B separated by 2a, with center O. Let P be a point with polar coordinates (r, &theta;), where r >> a.<br>Let distances of P from +q and -q be r<sub>1</sub> and r<sub>2</sub>.<br><br><b>2. Distance Approximations:</b><br>Draw perpendiculars from A and B onto line OP:<br>r<sub>1</sub> &approx; r - a cos &theta;<br>r<sub>2</sub> &approx; r + a cos &theta;<br><br><b>3. Net Potential Calculation:</b><br>V = V<sub>+</sub> + V<sub>-</sub> = [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ 1 / r<sub>1</sub> - 1 / r<sub>2</sub> ]<br>V = [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ 1 / (r - a cos &theta;) - 1 / (r + a cos &theta;) ]<br>V = [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ (2 a cos &theta;) / (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;) ]<br><br>Since dipole moment p = q (2a) and r >> a (neglecting a<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta; compared to r<sup>2</sup>):<br><b>V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ (p cos &theta;) / r<sup>2</sup> ]</b>.<br><br><b>4. Special Cases:</b><br>- <b>Axial Line (&theta; = 0&deg;):</b> cos 0&deg; = 1 &rArr; <b>V<sub>axial</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [p / r<sup>2</sup>]</b>.<br>- <b>Equatorial Line (&theta; = 90&deg;):</b> cos 90&deg; = 0 &rArr; <b>V<sub>eq</sub> = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q88: Derive an expression for the capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor with a dielectric slab of thickness t (t < d) inserted between its plates.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Setup:</b><br>Consider a parallel plate capacitor of plate area A and separation d carrying charges &plusmn;Q and surface charge densities &plusmn;&sigma; = &plusmn;Q/A.<br>In vacuum, electric field between plates is E<sub>0</sub> = &sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A).<br><br><b>2. Effect of Dielectric Slab:</b><br>When a dielectric slab of thickness t and dielectric constant K is inserted, the electric field inside the slab is reduced to E = E<sub>0</sub> / K.<br>The field in the remaining space of thickness (d - t) remains E<sub>0</sub>.<br><br><b>3. Potential Difference Between Plates:</b><br>V = E<sub>0</sub> (d - t) + E t = E<sub>0</sub> (d - t) + (E<sub>0</sub> / K) t<br>V = E<sub>0</sub> [ (d - t) + (t / K) ] = [ Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A) ] [ d - t + (t / K) ].<br><br><b>4. Capacitance:</b><br>C = Q / V = Q / [ (Q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A)) (d - t + t/K) ]<br><b>C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / [ d - t + (t / K) ]</b>.<br><br><b>Special Case:</b> When dielectric completely fills the gap (t = d):<br>C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / (d/K) = <b>K (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d) = K C<sub>0</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q89: Derive an expression for the electric field intensity on the axial line of an electric dipole. Hence, deduce the expression for a short dipole.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Setup:</b><br>Consider an electric dipole of charges -q and +q separated by 2a with center O. Let P be a point on the axial line at distance r from center O.<br><br><b>2. Field Magnitudes:</b><br>Distance of P from +q is (r - a): E<sub>+</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [q / (r - a)<sup>2</sup>] (directed along OP away from +q).<br>Distance of P from -q is (r + a): E<sub>-</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [q / (r + a)<sup>2</sup>] (directed towards -q along PO).<br><br><b>3. Net Axial Field:</b><br>E<sub>axial</sub> = E<sub>+</sub> - E<sub>-</sub> = [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ 1 / (r - a)<sup>2</sup> - 1 / (r + a)<sup>2</sup> ]<br>E<sub>axial</sub> = [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ (r + a)<sup>2</sup> - (r - a)<sup>2</sup> ] / (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ 4 a r ] / (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup>.<br><br>Substitute p = q (2a):<br><b>E<sub>axial</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ 2 p r / (r<sup>2</sup> - a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ]</b>.<br><br><b>4. Short Dipole Approximation (r >> a):</b><br><b>E<sub>axial</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ 2 p / r<sup>3</sup> ]</b>, directed parallel to dipole moment vector <b>p</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q90: Derive an expression for the electric field intensity on the equatorial line of an electric dipole.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Setup:</b><br>Consider dipole charges -q and +q separated by 2a. Let point Q be on the perpendicular bisector at distance r from center O.<br>Distance of Q from each charge is d = &radic;(r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>).<br>Magnitude E<sub>+</sub> = E<sub>-</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [q / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>)].<br><br><b>2. Resolving Components:</b><br>Vertical components E<sub>+</sub> sin &theta; and E<sub>-</sub> sin &theta; cancel out.<br>Horizontal components add up in the direction opposite to <b>p</b>:<br>E<sub>eq</sub> = 2 E cos &theta; = 2 [q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>))] [ a / &radic;(r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>) ]<br>E<sub>eq</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ (2 a q) / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup> ].<br><br>Substitute p = q (2a):<br><b>E<sub>eq</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ p / (r<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup> ]</b>.<br>For short dipole (r >> a): <b>E<sub>eq</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ p / r<sup>3</sup> ]</b>, directed anti-parallel to <b>p</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q91: State Gauss's Theorem in electrostatics. Using Gauss's Law, derive an expression for the electric field intensity due to an infinitely long straight uniformly charged wire.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Statement:</b><br>Gauss's Theorem states that total outward electric flux through any closed Gaussian surface in free space equals 1/&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> times net enclosed charge: &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.<br><br><b>2. Derivation for Infinite Wire (&lambda;):</b><br>Consider a long thin wire carrying uniform linear charge density &lambda;.<br>Construct a coaxial cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius r and length L centered on the wire.<br>Net enclosed charge: q<sub>enclosed</sub> = &lambda; L.<br><br>Flux through the 3 faces:<br>- Top circular face: <b>E</b> &perp; d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>1</sub> = 0.<br>- Bottom circular face: <b>E</b> &perp; d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>2</sub> = 0.<br>- Curved surface: <b>E</b> || d<b>A</b> &rArr; &Phi;<sub>3</sub> = E &int; dA = E (2&pi; r L).<br><br>Total Flux &Phi;<sub>total</sub> = E (2&pi; r L).<br>Applying Gauss's Law:<br>E (2&pi; r L) = &lambda; L / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <b>E = &lambda; / (2&pi; &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q92: Using Gauss's Law, derive an expression for the electric field intensity due to an infinite plane sheet of charge with uniform surface charge density &sigma;.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Setup:</b><br>Consider an infinite thin non-conducting plane sheet with uniform surface charge density &sigma;. Electric field lines emerge perpendicularly outwards on both sides.<br>Construct a cylindrical pillbox Gaussian surface of cross-sectional area A piercing the sheet perpendicularly.<br><br><b>2. Enclosed Charge & Flux:</b><br>Enclosed charge q<sub>enclosed</sub> = &sigma; A.<br>Flux through curved surface = 0 (since <b>E</b> is parallel to surface).<br>Flux through left and right flat end faces = E A + E A = 2 E A.<br><br><b>3. Applying Gauss's Law:</b><br>2 E A = &sigma; A / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub><br><b>E = &sigma; / (2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)</b>.<br>The electric field intensity is completely independent of distance from the sheet.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q93: Using Gauss's Law, derive expressions for the electric field intensity due to a uniformly charged thin spherical shell of radius R carrying charge Q: (i) outside the shell (r &ge; R), and (ii) inside the shell (r < R).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Case I: Outside the Shell (r &ge; R):</b><br>Construct a concentric spherical Gaussian surface of radius r &ge; R.<br>By spherical symmetry, <b>E</b> is uniform and radial. &Phi; = &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = E (4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>).<br>Enclosed charge = Q.<br>By Gauss's Law: E (4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>) = Q / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <b>E<sub>out</sub> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [Q / r<sup>2</sup>]</b>.<br><br><b>2. Case II: Inside the Shell (r < R):</b><br>Construct a concentric spherical Gaussian surface of radius r < R.<br>Since all charge resides entirely on the outer surface of the shell, enclosed charge is zero (q<sub>enclosed</sub> = 0).<br>By Gauss's Law: E (4&pi; r<sup>2</sup>) = 0 / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <b>E<sub>in</sub> = 0</b>.<br>This fundamental result proves electrostatic shielding.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q94: Derive an expression for the electrostatic energy stored in a charged parallel plate capacitor of capacitance C charged to potential difference V. Hence, find the energy density.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Energy Derivation:</b><br>Let at any instant of charging, charge on capacitor be q and potential difference be v = q / C.<br>Small work done in transferring additional charge dq is: dW = v dq = (q / C) dq.<br><br>Total work done in charging from q = 0 to q = Q:<br>W = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>Q</sup> (q / C) dq = (1 / C) [ q<sup>2</sup> / 2 ]<sub>0</sub><sup>Q</sup> = <b>Q<sup>2</sup> / (2 C)</b>.<br>Substituting Q = C V:<br><b>U = 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 Q V = Q<sup>2</sup> / (2C)</b>.<br><br><b>2. Energy Density (u):</b><br>Substitute C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d and V = E d:<br>U = 1/2 (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d) (E d)<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup> (A d).<br>Since volume of space between plates is Volume = A d:<br>Energy Density <b>u = U / (A d) = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup> [J m<sup>-3</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q95: Explain with diagrams and mathematical formulation the behavior of a conductor placed in an electrostatic field, detailing five fundamental properties.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Net Electric Field Inside is Zero (E = 0):</b> Mobile free electrons drift until internal field cancels external field completely.<br><b>2. Static Charge Resides Entirely on Surface:</b> By Gauss's Law, since E = 0 inside, net enclosed charge is zero.<br><b>3. Electric Field at Surface is Strictly Normal:</b> If tangential field existed, charges would flow; magnitude is E = &sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.<br><b>4. Electrostatic Potential is Constant Throughout:</b> Since E = -dV/dr = 0, potential V is uniform throughout the interior and on the surface.<br><b>5. Electric Field is Zero in Interior Cavity (Electrostatic Shielding):</b> Any hollow cavity inside a conductor is completely isolated from external fields.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q96: Explain the loss of electrostatic energy when two charged capacitors are connected together in parallel.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. System Description:</b><br>Let capacitors C<sub>1</sub> and C<sub>2</sub> be charged to potentials V<sub>1</sub> and V<sub>2</sub>.<br>Initial Total Energy: U<sub>i</sub> = 1/2 C<sub>1</sub> V<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + 1/2 C<sub>2</sub> V<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br><br><b>2. Common Potential:</b><br>When connected in parallel, common potential is V = (C<sub>1</sub> V<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> V<sub>2</sub>) / (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>).<br>Final Total Energy: U<sub>f</sub> = 1/2 (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>) V<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 (C<sub>1</sub> V<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> V<sub>2</sub>)<sup>2</sup> / (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>).<br><br><b>3. Energy Loss (&Delta;U):</b><br>&Delta;U = U<sub>i</sub> - U<sub>f</sub> = <b>[ C<sub>1</sub> C<sub>2</sub> (V<sub>1</sub> - V<sub>2</sub>)<sup>2</sup> ] / [ 2 (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>) ]</b>.<br>Since (V<sub>1</sub> - V<sub>2</sub>)<sup>2</sup> &ge; 0, &Delta;U is always positive. This energy is dissipated as heat in connecting wires and electromagnetic radiation.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q97: Derive the expression for electric field and potential due to a uniformly charged thin spherical shell at all points: (i) outside (r > R), (ii) on the surface (r = R), and (iii) inside (r < R). Sketch graphs of E vs r and V vs r.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Electric Field E(r):</b><br>- Outside (r &ge; R): E = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / r<sup>2</sup>) &prop; 1/r<sup>2</sup>.<br>- Surface (r = R): E = &sigma; / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.<br>- Inside (r < R): E = 0.<br><br><b>2. Electric Potential V(r):</b><br>- Outside (r &ge; R): V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / r) &prop; 1/r.<br>- Inside and on surface (r &le; R): Since E = 0 inside, V is constant: V = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (Q / R).<br><br><b>3. Graphical Behavior:</b><br>- E vs r: Zero from 0 to R, jumps to maximum at R, decays as 1/r<sup>2</sup> for r > R.<br>- V vs r: Constant horizontal line from 0 to R, decays as 1/r for r > R.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q98: Derive an expression for the potential energy of a system of three point charges q<sub>1</sub>, q<sub>2</sub>, and q<sub>3</sub> placed at position vectors r<sub>1</sub>, r<sub>2</sub>, and r<sub>3</sub> in free space.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Work in bringing q<sub>1</sub> from infinity to r<sub>1</sub>:</b><br>Since no field exists initially: W<sub>1</sub> = 0.<br><br><b>2. Work in bringing q<sub>2</sub> from infinity to r<sub>2</sub>:</b><br>Potential at r<sub>2</sub> due to q<sub>1</sub> is V<sub>1</sub>(r<sub>2</sub>) = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (q<sub>1</sub> / r<sub>12</sub>).<br>W<sub>2</sub> = q<sub>2</sub> V<sub>1</sub>(r<sub>2</sub>) = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub> / r<sub>12</sub>].<br><br><b>3. Work in bringing q<sub>3</sub> from infinity to r<sub>3</sub>:</b><br>Potential at r<sub>3</sub> due to q<sub>1</sub> and q<sub>2</sub> is V<sub>12</sub>(r<sub>3</sub>) = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ q<sub>1</sub> / r<sub>13</sub> + q<sub>2</sub> / r<sub>23</sub> ].<br>W<sub>3</sub> = q<sub>3</sub> V<sub>12</sub>(r<sub>3</sub>) = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>3</sub> / r<sub>13</sub> + q<sub>2</sub> q<sub>3</sub> / r<sub>23</sub> ].<br><br><b>4. Total Potential Energy:</b><br>U = W<sub>1</sub> + W<sub>2</sub> + W<sub>3</sub> = <b>[1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] [ (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub> / r<sub>12</sub>) + (q<sub>2</sub> q<sub>3</sub> / r<sub>23</sub>) + (q<sub>3</sub> q<sub>1</sub> / r<sub>31</sub>) ]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q99: Discuss the effect of introducing a dielectric slab of dielectric constant K into a parallel plate capacitor under two conditions: (i) when battery remains connected, and (ii) when battery is disconnected.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. When Battery Remains Connected (V = constant):</b><br>- Potential Difference: V' = V (constant).<br>- Capacitance: C' = K C<sub>0</sub> (increases by K).<br>- Charge on Plates: Q' = C' V = K (C<sub>0</sub> V) = K Q<sub>0</sub> (increases by K).<br>- Electric Field: E' = V / d = E<sub>0</sub> (constant).<br>- Energy Stored: U' = 1/2 C' V<sup>2</sup> = K (1/2 C<sub>0</sub> V<sup>2</sup>) = K U<sub>0</sub> (increases by K).<br><br><b>2. When Battery is Disconnected (Q = constant):</b><br>- Charge: Q' = Q<sub>0</sub> (conserved).<br>- Capacitance: C' = K C<sub>0</sub> (increases by K).<br>- Potential Difference: V' = Q / C' = V<sub>0</sub> / K (decreases by K).<br>- Electric Field: E' = E<sub>0</sub> / K (decreases by K).<br>- Energy Stored: U' = Q<sup>2</sup> / (2 C') = U<sub>0</sub> / K (decreases by K).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF512F; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.55;\">Q100: Provide an exhaustive master synthesis of JKBOSE / NCERT Unit I: Electrostatics (09 Marks) summarizing core principles, symmetry laws, and technological applications.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 81, 47, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF512F; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF512F; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Detailed Derivation & Theoretical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Fundamental Electrostatic Principles:</b><br>- Quantization: q = &plusmn;n e (e = 1.602 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> C).<br>- Coulomb's Law: <b>F</b> = [1 / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] (q<sub>1</sub> q<sub>2</sub> / r<sup>2</sup>) <b>r̂</b>.<br>- Superposition: <b>F</b><sub>net</sub> = &Sigma; <b>F</b><sub>i</sub>.<br><br><b>2. Fields and Potentials:</b><br>- Field: <b>E</b> = <b>F</b> / q<sub>0</sub>; Relation: <b>E = - &nabla;V</b> (E = -dV/dr).<br>- Dipole: <b>p</b> = q (2<b>a</b>); Torque <b>&tau;</b> = <b>p</b> &times; <b>E</b>; Energy U = -<b>p</b> &sdot; <b>E</b>.<br>- Potential: V = Q / (4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> r); Equipotentials are normal to <b>E</b>.<br><br><b>3. Gauss's Law & Capacitance:</b><br>- Gauss's Law: &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>.<br>- Parallel Plate Capacitor: C<sub>0</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / d, with dielectric slab C = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A / [d - t + t/K].<br>- Combinations: 1/C<sub>s</sub> = &Sigma; 1/C<sub>i</sub>; C<sub>p</sub> = &Sigma; C<sub>i</sub>.<br>- Stored Energy: U = 1/2 C V<sup>2</sup>; Energy Density u = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup>.<br><br><b>4. Applications:</b> Electrostatic shielding, Van de Graaff accelerators, defibrillators, photocopiers, and touchscreens.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";
